/* lenormand-text.js — classic script; assigns to window.TD. No ES modules, so file:// works.
   Texts for lenormand.html: lnm:<card> (36 cards) and lnmpair:<a>|<b> (all 630 pairs, ids in deck order). 666 texts. Written fresh for this demo; a live site edits or replaces them.
   Built by ../_astro-corpus-work/lenormand/build-lenormand.js. */
window.TD = window.TD || {};
window.TD.LENORMAND_TEXT = {"lnm:rider":"A horseman rides in with a message in hand, and that is the whole card: movement, news, someone arriving. It is quick and light, more like a text that pings than a long letter. In a question it often points to a development that is about to show up, or a person who turns up sooner than expected. Do not expect weight from it. The Rider tells you how fast something moves, not how important it is. Tip: keep your phone charged and your calendar loose, and answer promptly when something arrives.",
"lnm:clover":"Small luck lives here: the parking spot right outside, the sale that ends tomorrow, the stranger who holds the door. The Clover is a four-leaf find, charming but short. Its window is narrow, so the card asks you to notice a chance and use it while it lasts. In a question it suggests a brief opening or a bit of good fortune, never a permanent arrangement. Tip: say yes to the cheerful little opportunity, and do not build a whole plan on top of it.",
"lnm:ship":"A sailing ship crosses open water, carrying goods and people from one shore to another. The card speaks of distance, trade, travel and things that arrive slowly from somewhere else. In everyday questions it can mean a trip, a long-distance connection, a business that reaches beyond your own town, or a wish to get away. It also hints at a stretch of time between leaving and landing. Tip: check the route before you set sail, and allow generous time for what you are waiting on.",
"lnm:house":"Four walls, a roof and a door you can close: the House is the card of home and what keeps you steady. It covers family, the place you live and the sense of being safe inside your own life. In a question it often asks what needs protecting or tending close to home, or whether a situation feels solid underfoot. It can also describe an established, dependable setup. Tip: look after the foundations first, such as the routine, the household and the people at your table.",
"lnm:tree":"A tall tree with deep roots stands for slow, steady growth and the general wellbeing of body and spirit. It is long-lived and patient, and it does not hurry. In a question it points to things that develop over years: a skill, a family line, a habit of caring for yourself. It asks how well rooted you feel. It speaks of wellbeing in the broad sense, never of a condition. Tip: give the thing you care about steady water and sunlight, and resist digging it up to check progress.",
"lnm:clouds":"Grey weather rolls over the sky and the view blurs. The Clouds card stands for doubt, mixed signals and a situation where you cannot yet see clearly. It tends to be temporary, since clouds move on. In a question it can mean unclear facts, a mood that colours your judgement, or a conversation where nobody quite says what they mean. Tip: hold off on big decisions until the air clears, write down what you actually know, and ask a direct question rather than guessing.",
"lnm:snake":"A winding path with a few too many turns: the Snake is the card of complication, the long way round and the clever, slippery element in a situation. It can describe a tangled problem, a rival, or a person who is hard to read. Its energy is not always hostile, but it is rarely straightforward. In a question it asks where the story has become more twisty than it needs to be. Tip: slow down, read the fine print, and take the detour as information rather than an insult.",
"lnm:coffin":"A closed box, a lid, a full stop. The Coffin is read as an ending, a pause or closure in everyday terms, such as a project wrapping up, a chapter filing itself away or a necessary rest after a long push. It speaks of seasons closing, nothing more. In a question it often shows that something has run its course and needs to be put down properly. Tip: mark the ending on purpose, with a clean-up, a thank-you or a quiet evening, so that what comes next has room.",
"lnm:bouquet":"Someone has gathered flowers and handed them over. The Bouquet is the card of gifts, pleasure and being appreciated. It speaks of kindness freely offered, a nice invitation, a compliment that lands, or simply a day that is pleasant to be in. In a question it points to warmth and generosity in the situation, and sometimes to your own talent for making others feel welcome. Tip: accept the kind gesture without arguing with it, and pass a little of it on.",
"lnm:scythe":"A blade swings once and the field is cleared. The Scythe stands for a sudden cut, a sharp decision and something finished in a single stroke. Think of the email that ends a dithering debate, or the moment you finally say no. It can be clean and freeing, but it asks for care, since a fast cut leaves little room to undo it. Tip: before you act, check that your blade is aimed at the right thing, and then commit.",
"lnm:whip":"Rhythmic, repeated, sometimes sharp: the Whip is the card of conflict that goes round and round, and of effort applied again and again. It might be the same argument at the same dinner table, or a workout routine you keep up through sheer will. It can be about friction, but also about discipline and training. In a question it asks where you keep repeating a pattern. Tip: change the approach rather than raising the volume, and take a break from the cycle.",
"lnm:birds":"Two birds on a branch, chattering away. The Birds card is about talk, small worries, nerves and the buzz of two people or two things in conversation. It covers phone calls, gossip, a flurry of messages and the jittery feeling before a speech. In a question it often marks a talkative pair, or a situation where much is said and little settled. Tip: keep conversations short and clear, and do not let background chatter set your mood for the day.",
"lnm:child":"A small figure stands at the start of the path, curious and unguarded. The Child is the card of new beginnings, small things and a fresh, innocent approach. It can describe a young person, but more often a project in its first week, a trial run or a plain, trusting attitude. In a question it asks you to treat something as new and not yet proven. Tip: start small, ask the simple questions without embarrassment, and give a young idea time before judging it.",
"lnm:fox":"A fox slips through the hedge, alert and resourceful. The Fox is the card of cunning, self-interest and work, especially work done by wit. It can describe a person looking out for themselves, a job with a hint of politics, or your own need to be shrewd. In a question it raises the matter of whether everything is as it appears. Tip: double-check the details, keep your own interests in view, and use your cleverness fairly, so that it works for you and not against your name.",
"lnm:bear":"A large, calm animal that protects its territory. The Bear is the card of strength, resources and a guardian figure, such as a boss, a parent or a mentor with weight to throw around. It can also mean your own substance: reserves of energy, skills and influence. In a question it asks whether you are using strength well or holding tightly to it. Tip: be generous with your protection, and keep an eye on appetite, since the Bear can take more than it needs.",
"lnm:stars":"A clear night sky with points of light to steer by. The Stars card stands for hope, guidance and clarity, a sense that there is a direction worth following. It is often linked with ideas, goals and the inner conviction that something is possible. In a question it points to a bright, open prospect, and asks you to keep your eyes on the larger pattern. Tip: write down your guiding aim, check it against your next small step, and trust the long view over the daily noise.",
"lnm:stork":"A stork glides in on broad wings, carrying change. The Stork is the card of improvement, relocation and gentle transition, such as a house move, a new routine or a pleasant shift in how things run. It tends to be a change for the better and a slow glide rather than a crash landing. In a question it signals that a situation is moving toward something different. Tip: prepare the nest in advance, sort what you plan to keep, and welcome the new arrangement without clinging to the old.",
"lnm:dog":"A loyal animal waits by the door and walks beside you. The Dog is the card of friendship, trust and dependable support. It points to the friend who shows up with a casserole, the colleague who covers for you, or the steady companion you can count on. In a question it suggests that loyalty is present, or asks whether you are offering it. Tip: lean on the people who have earned your trust, and return the favour in small, regular ways.",
"lnm:tower":"A tall, solitary building stands apart from the street. The Tower is the card of institutions, authority and a deliberate distance from the crowd. It can mean a big organisation, such as a bank, a school or a council, or a person who guards their space and keeps their own counsel. In a question it points to formality, rules and a view from above, and sometimes to loneliness. Tip: respect the process when dealing with institutions, and make sure that your private space has a door you can open.",
"lnm:garden":"A public park with paths and benches, open to anyone. The Garden is the card of social life, gatherings and being seen by many people. It covers parties, networks, events and the wider community. In a question it often asks about your audience: who is around, who knows you and who might you meet. Tip: show up where people gather, such as a class, a meetup or a family barbecue, and let the connection grow at its own pace rather than forcing a pitch.",
"lnm:mountain":"A great wall of rock fills the horizon. The Mountain is the card of obstacles, delay and a big challenge that cannot be wished away. It might be a stalled application, a slow-moving person, or a goal that needs steady climbing. It is not a permanent block, but it does ask for patience and a plan. In a question it shows where the resistance sits. Tip: break the climb into camps, rest properly, and look for the path around the steepest face.",
"lnm:crossroads":"A road splits into three, and a signpost leans in the wind. The Crossroads is the card of choices, options and the pause before picking a direction. It shows up when more than one path is open and each has something to offer. In a question it asks you to weigh the options honestly, instead of drifting. Tip: list what each route costs and gives, talk it over with one trusted person, and then pick one, since lingering at the fork has its own price.",
"lnm:mice":"Small teeth nibble at the edges of the grain store. The Mice card stands for worry, wear and loss that happens by tiny amounts, such as a leaky budget, a draining habit or a thought that nags at three in the morning. Nothing dramatic occurs, yet the total adds up. In a question it asks what is slowly being used up. Tip: find the small leak, patch it early, and give your attention to the worry before it eats the whole pantry.",
"lnm:heart":"A simple red heart needs no translation. The Heart is the card of love, affection and warmth, whether between partners, family, friends or toward a pet. It points to what you care about and how openly you show it. In a question it often brings feeling to the front and asks you to lead with kindness. Tip: say the warm thing out loud, make time for the people you love, and let your decisions include the heart as well as the head.",
"lnm:ring":"A plain band, a circle with no end. The Ring is the card of commitment, agreements and cycles that return to their starting point. It covers contracts, partnerships, promises and any bond that is meant to hold. In a question it asks what you have bound yourself to, and whether that bond still fits. It can also describe a rhythm that repeats, like seasons. Tip: read what you sign, honour what you pledge, and renew an agreement on purpose rather than by habit.",
"lnm:book":"A closed book sits on the table, its cover giving nothing away. The Book is the card of secrets, study and knowledge not yet shared. It might be a subject you are learning, a plan kept quiet, or a fact someone has not disclosed. In a question it says that information is missing or not ready to share. Tip: do your reading, keep your own counsel until you know more, and ask for the missing chapter before drawing conclusions.",
"lnm:letter":"An envelope with a name on the front. The Letter is the card of messages, documents and anything put in writing: emails, forms, invoices, a note slipped under the door. It deals in the official and the explicit. In a question it points to paperwork, a written reply or the need to record something properly. Tip: put it in writing, keep a copy, and read the whole message before replying, including the small print at the bottom.",
"lnm:man":"A figure in the centre of the table stands for the person asking, or for someone important in the question. The Man card is a traditional name for this role, and it can stand for a person of any gender. Its nearby cards show how this person is placed: what surrounds them, what they carry and where they look. In a question it asks you to see the situation from that position. Tip: read the neighbouring cards as the current weather around that person.",
"lnm:woman":"Another figure sits in the centre of the table and stands for the person asking, or for someone significant in the question. The Woman card is a traditional name for this role and can stand for a person of any gender. The cards beside it describe that person's circumstances, mood and direction. In a question it invites you to look at who is at the heart of the matter. Tip: notice which neighbours bring support and which bring strain, and let that guide your next step.",
"lnm:lily":"White blooms rise on a calm pond. The Lily is the card of peace, maturity and quiet harmony. It points to a settled attitude, the wisdom that comes with years and a gentler pace of life. In a question it suggests calm, a respectful atmosphere and a situation that does not need force. Tip: lower the temperature, choose the patient answer, and let a long-standing steadiness do its work instead of stirring things up.",
"lnm:sun":"Bright light pours over everything and the shadows shrink. The Sun is the card of success, energy and warmth, one of the clearest and most hopeful in the deck. It stands for vitality, confidence and the sense that effort is being seen. In a question it points to a bright outlook and a time when things come easily into view. Tip: use the energy while it is high, share the credit generously, and spend some time outdoors.",
"lnm:moon":"A pale disc rises and the tide answers. The Moon is the card of recognition, feelings and intuition. It covers praise, reputation and the emotional undercurrent beneath a situation. In a question it asks what you feel, and whether the response you seek is honour or inner assurance. It can also describe moods that swell and fade. Tip: keep a journal of your hunches, notice the applause but do not live on it, and remember that feelings come in phases.",
"lnm:key":"A single key turns in a lock, and the door swings wide. The Key is the card of solutions, certainty and an opening that was waiting for the right fit. It is often about the missing piece: the answer, the access or the plain fact that settles the question. In a question it signals that something can be resolved. Tip: look for what unlocks the situation, such as one honest conversation or one overlooked detail, and use it with confidence.",
"lnm:fish":"Silver fish move together through deep water. The Fish is the card of money, business and the way resources flow. It speaks of trade, earning, abundance and the give-and-take of an enterprise. In a question it points to how value moves, and to how freely you let it. It offers no financial advice and promises no sum. Tip: keep your records tidy, let the current move without hoarding, and notice where your time and effort stream in or out.",
"lnm:anchor":"A heavy anchor sinks and the boat stops drifting. The Anchor is the card of stability, perseverance and the long term. It speaks of steady work, a secure footing and a commitment you hold through changing weather. In a question it points to something durable, or asks what keeps you grounded. Tip: stay with the plan that has served you, build on what is dependable, and be careful that holding firm does not turn into refusing to move at all.",
"lnm:cross":"Two beams meet, and something heavy is carried on the back. The Cross is the card of burden, duty and meaning. It covers obligations you did not choose, trials that test you, and also the sense of purpose that comes with carrying something worthwhile. In a question it asks what weight you are holding and why. Tip: share the load when you can, put down what is not yours, and honour the meaning in what you choose to keep.",
"lnmpair:rider|clover":"A quick message plus a small stroke of luck: the Rider and the Clover often point to a short, pleasant surprise that moves fast, like a text offering a spare ticket. The window is brief, so a prompt reply serves better than a week of deliberating.",
"lnmpair:rider|ship":"Word from far away. The Rider and the Ship read as news crossing a distance, such as a call from a relative abroad or a supplier updating a delivery. Check time zones and shipping details before reacting, since the message may be older than it looks.",
"lnmpair:rider|house":"Someone or something is heading toward your household. The Rider and the House can describe a visit from family, news about a home, or a lively change in routine indoors. Tidy up a little and keep the week flexible.",
"lnmpair:rider|tree":"Growth arrives by a messenger, in a slow-burning way. The Rider and the Tree can suggest news about wellbeing habits, a long project taking root, or a visit from someone who has known you for years. Take it in calmly rather than rushing a response.",
"lnmpair:rider|clouds":"Hazy news. The Rider and the Clouds describe a message that is unclear, half-explained or easy to misread, like an email with no real details. Ask a plain follow-up question before deciding what it means, and hold off on assumptions.",
"lnmpair:rider|snake":"A message with a twist in it. The Rider and the Snake suggest news that arrives sideways, perhaps from someone with a hidden agenda or through a roundabout route. Read it slowly, verify the source, and answer only what was actually asked.",
"lnmpair:rider|coffin":"News that closes something out. The Rider and the Coffin often point to a message bringing a pause, a finished chapter or a quiet period, such as a project winding down. Treat it as a clean stop, and give yourself room before starting the next thing.",
"lnmpair:rider|bouquet":"A kind gesture on its way. The Rider and the Bouquet suggest an invitation, a thank-you note or an unexpected gift turning up at your door. Accept it graciously, and consider sending a small appreciation of your own in return.",
"lnmpair:rider|scythe":"Fast news that demands a fast answer. The Rider and the Scythe describe a sudden message that cuts through a stalemate, like a deadline moved up overnight. Decide what you can and cannot change, then act cleanly instead of dragging it out.",
"lnmpair:rider|whip":"The same message keeps coming back. The Rider and the Whip suggest repeated contact, nagging follow-ups or a debate that circles through several rounds of replies. Set a clear boundary on when you respond, and keep your tone level.",
"lnmpair:rider|birds":"A flurry of chatter. The Rider and the Birds point to quick conversations, group texts and a slightly jittery mood around news. Pick one trusted voice to confirm the facts, and take a breath before you pass anything along.",
"lnmpair:rider|child":"A fresh, small beginning is approaching. The Rider and the Child can describe an introduction to a newcomer, a first message from a new contact, or a trial run of an idea. Stay curious and keep expectations modest and light.",
"lnmpair:rider|fox":"Pay attention to the motive behind the message. The Rider and the Fox suggest news tied to work, a clever pitch, or someone looking out for their own interest. Read the fine print, ask how it benefits the sender, and keep your own plan intact.",
"lnmpair:rider|bear":"Steady support is coming through a message. The Rider and the Bear point to news from a capable, protective figure, such as a mentor offering help or a bank confirming resources. Lean on it sensibly, and say thank you plainly.",
"lnmpair:rider|stars":"A hopeful signal arrives. The Rider and the Stars describe news that brings clarity or a sense of direction, like a reply that finally confirms your plan makes sense. Write down what became clear, and let it guide your next small step.",
"lnmpair:rider|stork":"News about a shift. The Rider and the Stork suggest a message tied to moving, changing jobs or improving a situation that has felt stuck. Start sorting what you want to carry forward, and let the change unfold at its own pace.",
"lnmpair:rider|dog":"A reliable friend reaches out. The Rider and the Dog describe a message from someone loyal, such as a longtime pal checking in or a colleague who backs you up. Return the favor with a prompt, warm reply.",
"lnmpair:rider|tower":"Formal word from an institution. The Rider and the Tower often point to a message from an office, a school, a regulator or someone who prefers distance. Expect a measured tone, keep copies of anything official, and answer in kind.",
"lnmpair:rider|garden":"An invitation to gather. The Rider and the Garden suggest news about a party, a community event or a message that spreads among many people. Show up if it appeals to you, and bring a little social energy.",
"lnmpair:rider|mountain":"News meets a wall. The Rider and the Mountain suggest a message that brings a delay, a refusal or a task that looks steeper than expected, like an approval that stalls for weeks. Break the climb into stages and pace yourself.",
"lnmpair:rider|crossroads":"A message that leaves you with options. The Rider and the Crossroads describe news that opens two or more paths, such as two offers arriving in the same week. List the pros and cons on paper before answering either one.",
"lnmpair:rider|mice":"Small worries nibbling at the edges. The Rider and the Mice suggest news that stirs anxiety bit by bit, like a string of minor notices about a shrinking budget item. Deal with the small things one at a time before they pile up.",
"lnmpair:rider|heart":"Warm words on the way. The Rider and the Heart point to an affectionate message, a call from someone who cares, or a visit full of tenderness. Let yourself enjoy it, and answer with the same sincerity you received.",
"lnmpair:rider|ring":"News about an agreement. The Rider and the Ring suggest a message tied to a contract, a pledge or a cycle coming back around, such as a renewal notice. Read every clause carefully and take your time before you sign anything.",
"lnmpair:rider|book":"News with something kept back. The Rider and the Book can point to a message about study, a course, or information that has not yet been fully revealed. Do some research first, and be patient while the whole picture comes together.",
"lnmpair:rider|letter":"Paperwork on the move. The Rider and the Letter stress written communication: an email, a form, a package of documents that must arrive and be filed. Keep a copy, check the dates, and reply in writing if you want a clear record.",
"lnmpair:rider|man":"Someone is moving toward the person asking. The Rider and the Man card describe the querent or a significant person receiving or delivering news, perhaps with a bit of urgency. Ask what that person needs to hear, and say it directly.",
"lnmpair:rider|woman":"A message is headed toward a central figure. The Rider and the Woman card describe the querent or a significant person getting a visit or an update, often with a touch of intuition. Listen first, and respond with care.",
"lnmpair:rider|lily":"A calm message arrives. The Rider and the Lily describe peaceful news, a mature voice offering perspective, or a gentle visit that settles the mood. Take your time, keep your tone kind, and let the quiet carry weight.",
"lnmpair:rider|sun":"Bright news is traveling your way. The Rider and the Sun suggest an encouraging message, a visitor with good energy, or a result that lifts spirits, like a positive review. Share it freely, and savor the warmth while it lasts.",
"lnmpair:rider|moon":"Feelings arrive with the news. The Rider and the Moon point to a message that stirs emotion or brings recognition, such as praise for work you did quietly. Notice your gut reaction before replying, and honor it.",
"lnmpair:rider|key":"A message that unlocks something. The Rider and the Key suggest news containing the answer you were waiting for, like confirmation of a date or the missing piece of a puzzle. Act on it with confidence, and keep the details handy.",
"lnmpair:rider|fish":"News about business and flow. The Rider and the Fish describe a message involving trade, income, or a deal moving along, such as an invoice or a client inquiry. Keep records tidy, and read every figure before you respond.",
"lnmpair:rider|anchor":"Word that supports the long game. The Rider and the Anchor suggest a message about steady, lasting matters, like confirmation of a stable job or a plan that keeps on course. Hold your position, and avoid knee-jerk reactions.",
"lnmpair:rider|cross":"A weighty message. The Rider and the Cross suggest news that carries duty, responsibility or a sense of meaning, such as a family obligation. Take a moment to ask what is truly yours to carry, and share the load where you can.",
"lnmpair:clover|ship":"A small chance tied to distance. The Clover and the Ship suggest a lucky break through travel or trade, like a cheap fare that appears for one afternoon. Check your calendar fast, and book only what you can honestly manage.",
"lnmpair:clover|house":"Good fortune close to home. The Clover and the House point to a pleasant surprise in the household, like a neighbor dropping by with an offer or an unexpected afternoon of peace. Enjoy it, and do not overbook the day.",
"lnmpair:clover|tree":"A little lift for your wellbeing. The Clover and the Tree suggest a short, fortunate stretch that supports growth, such as a good week for starting a walking habit. Plant the habit now, and let it root slowly.",
"lnmpair:clover|clouds":"Luck behind a haze. The Clover and the Clouds describe a chance that is hard to see clearly, like an offer with vague terms. Gather facts before grabbing it, and give the fog a little time to lift.",
"lnmpair:clover|snake":"A lucky break with a complication attached. The Clover and the Snake suggest a small opportunity that comes with a catch, a rival, or a roundabout route. Check who else is involved, and move with care rather than speed.",
"lnmpair:clover|coffin":"A small opening beside a closing door. The Clover and the Coffin suggest a brief bit of relief arriving as something winds down, like a tedious project wrapping up a day early. Take the short good moment gladly, and let the finished thing stay finished.",
"lnmpair:clover|bouquet":"Here is a light, pleasant surprise. The Clover and the Bouquet describe a small kindness that lands well, such as a coworker bringing you coffee on a rough morning. Accept it without analysing it, and pass a little appreciation along in return.",
"lnmpair:clover|scythe":"Quick choices matter now. The Clover and the Scythe point to a short window that closes fast, like a sale ending tonight or a seat that gets taken. If something feels right, a clean decision beats weeks of circling. Cut the extra deliberation.",
"lnmpair:clover|whip":"Expect a bit of luck that still asks for effort. The Clover and the Whip read as a small break inside a repetitive or heated stretch, like a bickering family dinner that ends with one real laugh. Keep your energy steady and take the lull.",
"lnmpair:clover|birds":"Lively chatter with a lucky edge. The Clover and the Birds suggest a short, cheerful conversation, perhaps a quick call or a pair of friends trading jokes. A little nervous energy hums underneath. Say the light thing out loud and enjoy the exchange.",
"lnmpair:clover|child":"Something small and fresh is within reach. The Clover and the Child describe a modest beginning, like trying a new class once or starting a tiny habit. Keep expectations playful. A first try carries more charm when nobody is grading it.",
"lnmpair:clover|fox":"A lucky break that deserves a second look. The Clover and the Fox hint at a quick opportunity at work with a self-interested angle, such as a favor offered with strings. Enjoy the chance, read the details, and stay a little savvy.",
"lnmpair:clover|bear":"Steady strength meets a small piece of good fortune. The Clover and the Bear suggest a helpful ally or ample resources turning up when needed, like a generous relative covering a ride. Accept support gracefully and keep your own footing too.",
"lnmpair:clover|stars":"Hope arrives with a spark of timing. The Clover and the Stars point to a bright moment of clarity, perhaps an idea that falls into place on a walk home. Write it down while it is vivid, then follow the direction it suggests.",
"lnmpair:clover|stork":"A gentle improvement is in motion. The Clover and the Stork read as a small, welcome shift, like a better schedule or a short move across town that eases the commute. Welcome the change lightly and let it settle before judging it.",
"lnmpair:clover|dog":"A trusted friend brings a little luck. The Clover and the Dog suggest loyalty showing up in a modest, timely way, like someone saving you a seat or passing along a useful tip. Say thank you, and be as reliable for them.",
"lnmpair:clover|tower":"A brief opening inside something formal. The Clover and the Tower point to a small win with an office, school or agency, like a form approved on the first try. Authority can feel distant, so be polite, prepared and short on your requests.",
"lnmpair:clover|garden":"A pleasant turn in a social setting. The Clover and the Garden suggest a fun gathering, a friendly crowd or an event where a chance meeting goes well. Show up, say hello to a stranger, and let the afternoon be unplanned.",
"lnmpair:clover|mountain":"A quick bit of luck against a large obstacle. The Clover and the Mountain show a short reprieve in a slow climb, like a rescheduled deadline or a clear stretch of road. Rest in it briefly, then keep moving toward the bigger task.",
"lnmpair:clover|crossroads":"Several small chances sit side by side. The Clover and the Crossroads picture a minor choice with more than one decent route, like two good invitations on the same evening. Pick the one that feels lighter. Neither path is wasted.",
"lnmpair:clover|mice":"Luck is thin, and worry nibbles at it. The Clover and the Mice show a small gain being eroded by fretting or slow leaks, like a good week undone by checking a bank app ten times. Name the worry once, then put it down.",
"lnmpair:clover|heart":"Warmth with a light touch. The Clover and the Heart suggest a sweet moment of affection, like an unexpected text from someone who cares. It does not need to be a grand gesture to count. Notice it, enjoy it, and answer kindly.",
"lnmpair:clover|ring":"A small chance tied to a promise. The Clover and the Ring describe a short window around a commitment or agreement, like a quick chance to sign up before a deadline closes. Read what you are agreeing to, then act while the door is open.",
"lnmpair:clover|book":"A lucky glimpse of something hidden. The Clover and the Book suggest a fortunate bit of learning, like a lesson that clicks or a stray fact that answers a question. Some details are still out of sight, so stay curious and keep studying.",
"lnmpair:clover|letter":"Good news in a small package. The Clover and the Letter point to a short, friendly message, a quick email or note that lightens the day. Reply promptly while the tone is warm, and keep it brief. Short words suit this moment.",
"lnmpair:clover|man":"Luck gathers around a person. The Clover and the Man card, which stands for you or a significant person of any gender, suggest a cheerful stretch for that person, like a good mood after a small win. Join in, and let their ease be catching.",
"lnmpair:clover|woman":"A bright moment for a particular person. The Clover and the Woman card, standing for you or a significant person of any gender, suggest easy confidence, a lucky coincidence or a smile at the right time. Share the good mood and keep things light.",
"lnmpair:clover|lily":"Calm and a little luck arrive together. The Clover and the Lily describe a quiet, mature kind of ease, like a gentle afternoon with no demands on it. Do not hurry to fill the space. Peace is the real prize here.",
"lnmpair:clover|sun":"A bright, brief burst of success. The Clover and the Sun suggest energy running high, perhaps a good result on a task you almost skipped. Use that warmth while it lasts, and share the credit with whoever helped.",
"lnmpair:clover|moon":"A moment of recognition arrives softly. The Clover and the Moon point to a fleeting good feeling, such as a compliment that touches you or a hunch that proves right. Trust the gut sense, and let the feeling be there without forcing a reason.",
"lnmpair:clover|key":"A small door swings open. The Clover and the Key suggest a quick solution turning up, like finding the missing document or hearing the one sentence that explains everything. Use the opening promptly, and check that the answer truly fits your problem.",
"lnmpair:clover|fish":"Flow with a lucky edge in business matters. The Clover and the Fish suggest a small, pleasant bit of movement in trade or earning, like a client paying early. Treat it as a nice bonus, and keep your usual care with accounts.",
"lnmpair:clover|anchor":"A little luck with staying power. The Clover and the Anchor show a good moment that can be steadied into something lasting, like a brief bit of encouragement that keeps a habit alive. Keep showing up, one ordinary day after another.",
"lnmpair:clover|cross":"A lighter moment within a heavy stretch. The Clover and the Cross describe a short break inside a duty or burden, like an hour of rest during a demanding season of caregiving. Take the breather, and remember it can give meaning to the weight.",
"lnmpair:ship|house":"Distance meets home. The Ship and the House suggest travel toward or away from the family base, such as a long trip back for the holidays or a relative arriving from afar. Plan a comfortable landing, since a homecoming is easier with a settled routine.",
"lnmpair:ship|tree":"Growth that needs room to roam. The Ship and the Tree point to wellbeing connected with movement, like a walking trip that restores energy or a long-term goal pursued over distance. Keep your roots in mind while you travel, and pace yourself.",
"lnmpair:ship|clouds":"Plans drift into fog. The Ship and the Clouds show a trip or trade clouded by doubt, perhaps a delayed flight or an unclear contract from another city. Wait for clearer information before committing, and keep options open until the haze lifts.",
"lnmpair:ship|snake":"A detour appears on the route. The Ship and the Snake suggest a complication in travel or distant dealings, like a rival firm in another market or a winding reroute. Stay alert, double-check the details, and avoid shortcuts that look tempting.",
"lnmpair:ship|coffin":"A journey pauses or concludes. The Ship and the Coffin point to a trip winding down, a shipment finished or a long-distance arrangement closing quietly. Treat the pause as a chance to rest and review. Something new can leave the harbor later.",
"lnmpair:ship|bouquet":"Pleasure arrives from far away. The Ship and the Bouquet suggest a lovely gift sent from a distance, or a trip that feels like a treat, such as a weekend away with good company. Savor it fully, and send thanks across the miles.",
"lnmpair:ship|scythe":"A sudden turn in travel or trade. The Ship and the Scythe describe a quick cut to a plan, like a canceled booking or a quick decision to leave a distant job behind. Move cleanly, keep a backup in mind, and accept the shorter route.",
"lnmpair:ship|whip":"A long road with repeated effort. The Ship and the Whip suggest strain over distance, such as a commute that wears thin or repeated arguments with a faraway partner by video call. Build a steadier rhythm, and speak plainly when the topic keeps returning.",
"lnmpair:ship|birds":"Talk crosses the water. The Ship and the Birds picture nervous chatter about travel or a flurry of calls between two far-flung places, like friends coordinating a reunion from different time zones. Settle the details in writing, and breathe between messages.",
"lnmpair:ship|child":"A small beginning far from home. The Ship and the Child suggest a first step into new territory, like a first trip abroad or a fresh venture selling to a new region. Start modestly, stay curious, and let the early efforts be imperfect.",
"lnmpair:ship|fox":"Work with a distant, calculating edge. The Ship and the Fox point to dealings across distance that call for savvy, like an overseas supplier with unclear terms. Verify the facts, keep records, and trust your sharp instincts about who is straight with you.",
"lnmpair:ship|bear":"Distance backed by muscle. The Ship and the Bear together point to a venture that has real weight behind it, or a faraway person who acts as your protector. In a question about work abroad, it asks whether you have the reserves for the trip. Tip: check your resources before booking anything.",
"lnmpair:ship|stars":"A far horizon with a clear light over it. The Ship and the Stars read as guidance for a journey, or hope that arrives from somewhere else. Think of a long-distance plan that finally gets a clear direction after a confusing stretch. Tip: write down the one goal you keep steering toward.",
"lnmpair:ship|stork":"Movement upon movement. The Ship and the Stork suggest a real relocation, a changed route, or an improvement that comes through travel. A typical example is a job offer in another city that makes the whole shape of your week different. Tip: list what you would carry along and what you would leave.",
"lnmpair:ship|dog":"A loyal friend across the miles. The Ship and the Dog point to a trusted person who lives far away, or a dependable partner in trade. You might hear it when asking about a friend overseas who still answers every message. Tip: keep the contact warm with small, regular check-ins.",
"lnmpair:ship|tower":"Distance with a gatekeeper. The Ship and the Tower can describe travel through official channels, an overseas institution, or a lonely stretch away from everyone. Picture waiting at a border desk or studying alone in a foreign city. Tip: have your paperwork ready and accept some solitude as part of it.",
"lnmpair:ship|garden":"Faraway people gathering. The Ship and the Garden read as an event with guests who come from elsewhere, or a market that reaches a wider public. Think of an online shop that suddenly gets customers from other regions. Tip: show up where the crowd is and let your name travel.",
"lnmpair:ship|mountain":"A long road with a hill in the middle. The Ship and the Mountain suggest a delayed trip, a stalled shipment, or a plan that slows down before it moves again. A flight that gets pushed back is the plain picture. Tip: build slack into your schedule and plan around the wait.",
"lnmpair:ship|crossroads":"Several routes out. The Ship and the Crossroads ask which direction to sail, whether that is a city to move to or a market to enter. Someone weighing two overseas offers fits this pair. Tip: sketch the cost and the daily life of each path before you commit.",
"lnmpair:ship|mice":"A slow leak on a long voyage. The Ship and the Mice describe worry that nibbles at a faraway plan, or goods and energy lost in small amounts along the way. You might notice it in shipping costs creeping up. Tip: find the small drain and patch it early.",
"lnmpair:ship|heart":"Love at a distance. The Ship and the Heart are the classic long-distance romance, or affection that grows through letters and late calls. It can also show a heartfelt trip taken for the sake of someone dear. Tip: let the warmth show in what you say, since you cannot be there in person.",
"lnmpair:ship|ring":"A bond that crosses borders. The Ship and the Ring point to a contract with someone far away, an international partnership, or a commitment that involves travel. Think of an agreement signed by video call. Tip: read the terms about distance and timing before you agree.",
"lnmpair:ship|book":"Study from afar. The Ship and the Book suggest learning abroad, a hidden detail about a faraway place, or knowledge still on its way to you. A course taken online from another country is a fair example. Tip: dig into the unknown part before you set out.",
"lnmpair:ship|letter":"News from far off. The Ship and the Letter are a message or document crossing a long distance, such as a parcel with a note inside or an overseas email you have been waiting on. Tip: keep your inbox and mailbox in view, and answer promptly once it lands.",
"lnmpair:ship|man":"The person this card stands for is on the move. The Ship and the Man show the person asking, or someone significant, travelling, living away, or working with far places. A partner who often flies for work is one picture. Tip: ask where this person stands on being away.",
"lnmpair:ship|woman":"The person this card stands for is in motion. The Ship and the Woman show the person asking, or someone significant, who travels, lives elsewhere, or deals with distant matters. A sibling studying overseas fits. Tip: stay curious about their life there and share yours in return.",
"lnmpair:ship|lily":"A calm crossing. The Ship and the Lily read as a peaceful trip, a mature person from far away, or a long-distance matter that settles into harmony. Picture a quiet retreat by the sea. Tip: do not rush the route, and let the pace be gentle.",
"lnmpair:ship|sun":"Bright skies on the voyage. The Ship and the Sun point to successful travel, energy for an overseas project, or warm weather and a good mood on holiday. A business trip that goes well is the plain example. Tip: use the momentum to say yes to what stretches you.",
"lnmpair:ship|moon":"Feelings out on open water. The Ship and the Moon suggest recognition coming from far away, or intuition about a journey. An emotional homesickness can also show here. Tip: trust the quiet pull you feel about a place, and then test it with facts.",
"lnmpair:ship|key":"A way through the distance. The Ship and the Key point to a solution found by travelling, or a firm opening for a plan abroad. A visa sorted out after weeks of confusion is a fair picture. Tip: act on the clear answer instead of reopening the question.",
"lnmpair:ship|fish":"Trade on the move. The Ship and the Fish are imports, exports, and money that flows across distance. A small seller shipping goods to other countries fits well. Tip: keep your records tidy so the flow stays easy to follow.",
"lnmpair:ship|anchor":"A long game with steady footing. The Ship and the Anchor suggest staying power in something distant, or a trip meant to settle you in one place. Think of moving abroad for years and not just a season. Tip: choose the harbour you want to stay in before you leave.",
"lnmpair:ship|cross":"A journey with weight. The Ship and the Cross describe travel taken out of duty, or a faraway matter that carries meaning and effort. Caring for a relative in another city might show this. Tip: pace yourself and name what makes the trip worth it.",
"lnmpair:house|tree":"Roots in good soil. The House and the Tree read as a healthy, well-grounded home life, or a family line that keeps growing. A household that feels settled and nourishing fits. Tip: tend the daily habits that keep everyone steady, such as shared meals and enough sleep.",
"lnmpair:house|clouds":"A haze over the household. The House and the Clouds suggest confusion at home, unclear plans with family, or a passing rough patch. You might feel it when no one can agree on what is wrong. Tip: talk plainly, one subject at a time, until the air clears.",
"lnmpair:house|snake":"A twist in the domestic path. The House and the Snake point to a complication at home, a detour in a family plan, or a rival for your space. A tangled lease or a roommate dispute is a fair picture. Tip: keep your agreements in writing and your tone even.",
"lnmpair:house|coffin":"A chapter at home closing. The House and the Coffin read as a pause, a wrapping up, or the end of a living arrangement. It might be the last weekend in a rented flat before you leave. Tip: allow time to say goodbye to the place before the next one begins.",
"lnmpair:house|bouquet":"Home with a gift in it. The House and the Bouquet suggest hospitality, a pleasant surprise for the family, or a space that feels cared for. A friend brings flowers to a housewarming. Tip: say thank you out loud to the people who make home feel good.",
"lnmpair:house|scythe":"A quick cut in the household. The House and the Scythe point to a sudden decision about home, a clear-out, or a plan that is trimmed fast. Picture clearing a closet in one weekend. Tip: decide on what truly matters first, then cut with care.",
"lnmpair:house|whip":"Friction under the roof. The House and the Whip describe repeated arguments, chores that never end, or a push for effort at home. The same quarrel about the dishes is the plain example. Tip: change the system rather than repeating the fight.",
"lnmpair:house|birds":"Chatter around the table. The House and the Birds suggest lively family talk, nervous energy, or a pair who share the same roof. A busy kitchen with two voices going at once fits. Tip: breathe, and take the calls and chats one at a time.",
"lnmpair:house|child":"A fresh start at home. The Child and the House together point to something small and new in the household, such as a first apartment, a new routine, or a playful spirit in the family. Tip: begin gently and let the new thing find its feet before judging it.",
"lnmpair:house|fox":"Work done from home, with a sharp eye. The House and the Fox can describe a home business, or a need to look out for self-interest within the family. Someone who runs a side job from the spare room fits. Tip: keep roles and boundaries clear.",
"lnmpair:house|bear":"A strong base. The House and the Bear read as a protective household, a person who holds the family together, or real resources behind a home. A parent or elder who anchors holidays is one example. Tip: lean on that strength, and give some back.",
"lnmpair:house|stars":"Hope for the household. The House and the Stars suggest clarity about where to live, a guiding idea for family life, or a bright outlook on home. Someone finally seeing the plan for their space fits. Tip: pick one aim for the home and let it steer your choices.",
"lnmpair:house|stork":"A change of address, or a home that improves. The House and the Stork point to moving, renovating, or a household that shifts for the better. Think of repainting every room before moving in. Tip: take the change step by step and keep the essentials in one box.",
"lnmpair:house|dog":"A faithful presence at home. The House and the Dog suggest a trusted friend who feels like family, or loyalty within the household. A neighbour who keeps your spare key is the plain picture. Tip: show your trust by returning the favour in kind.",
"lnmpair:house|tower":"Space that is walled and quiet. The House and the Tower read as solitude at home, a formal property matter, or a household with strict rules. Someone living alone and liking it fits. Tip: decide whether the quiet restores you or leaves you shut in.",
"lnmpair:house|garden":"An open door for company. The House and the Garden point to guests, gatherings, and a home that welcomes the public. A neighbourhood party in the yard is the clear image. Tip: set the guest list and the end time so hosting stays enjoyable.",
"lnmpair:house|mountain":"A hurdle in the home picture. The House and the Mountain suggest a slow process around housing, a stubborn family issue, or a delay in settling. A purchase that drags on is one example. Tip: take it in small stages and count the progress you do make.",
"lnmpair:house|crossroads":"Several options for where to live. The House and the Crossroads ask a household question: stay, move, or share. Two apartments with different strengths is the classic picture. Tip: write down what each place gives your daily life, then choose with a clear head.",
"lnmpair:house|mice":"Small worries nibbling at the foundations of home life. The House and the Mice point to a leaky tap, a creeping bill or a tension that grows a little each week. Fix the minor thing early and say the awkward part out loud before it wears down everyone's patience.",
"lnmpair:house|heart":"Warmth under one roof. The House and the Heart describe a household where affection is easy to feel, or a longing to make a place feel loved. Think of cooking for family after a long week. Tip: say the kind thing out loud instead of assuming it is known.",
"lnmpair:house|ring":"A promise tied to a place. The House and the Ring suggest a lease signed together, a shared arrangement or a family commitment that comes round each year. Read the terms carefully, and ask whether everyone involved wants the same arrangement.",
"lnmpair:house|book":"Something unspoken in the family story. The House and the Book point to old records, a topic nobody raises at dinner, or learning done at home, like studying in the spare room. Tip: ask the quiet questions gently and listen to the answers.",
"lnmpair:house|letter":"News arriving at the door. The House and the Letter show paperwork, a family message or a note about where you live. A lease renewal or a card from relatives fits well. Tip: open it today and reply in writing so there is a record.",
"lnmpair:house|man":"You, or someone important, settled at the centre of the domestic sphere. The House and the Man card describe a person who anchors the household or wants a steadier base. Ask what that person needs to feel secure at home, and offer it plainly.",
"lnmpair:house|woman":"Someone at the heart of daily family life. The House and the Woman card describe the person asking, or a significant person, who keeps the household running or craves a calmer base. Example: the one who remembers every birthday. Tip: share the load.",
"lnmpair:house|lily":"A calm, mature household. The House and the Lily suggest peace at home, an older relative's wisdom, or a quiet routine that finally feels right. Sunday dinners without a rush are a good picture. Tip: protect the calm by keeping the schedule simple.",
"lnmpair:house|sun":"Bright, energetic home life. The House and the Sun point to a place full of daylight, good mood and shared plans, maybe a project like repainting together. Tip: use the momentum to finish the one domestic task you keep putting off.",
"lnmpair:house|moon":"Feelings run through the family. The House and the Moon describe a household where intuition matters, moods are noticed and someone may be recognised for quiet care. Picture the relative who always senses you are upset. Tip: name the feeling early.",
"lnmpair:house|key":"An answer to a domestic question. The House and the Key point to a practical solution about where to live, how to share space or a family disagreement that finally opens up. A clear talk about chores is a fair example. Tip: pick the simple fix.",
"lnmpair:house|fish":"Resources moving through the household. The House and the Fish suggest a family business, income earned from home or an easy exchange of help among relatives. A side project run from the spare room fits. Tip: keep a plain record of who contributes what.",
"lnmpair:house|anchor":"Long-term roots. The House and the Anchor describe a place or family tie built to last, and the patience needed to stay put. Staying in one neighbourhood for years is the picture. Tip: decide what you want to keep steady, then put your effort there.",
"lnmpair:house|cross":"A duty carried within the family. The House and the Cross point to caring for relatives, a heavy responsibility at home, or a sense of meaning in what you hold together. Tip: share the load, and notice where the burden is also a purpose.",
"lnmpair:tree|clouds":"Growth that feels foggy. The Tree and the Clouds describe a stretch where direction or wellbeing is unclear and doubt hangs around, like not knowing whether a new habit is helping. Tip: slow down, rest and wait for clearer light before deciding.",
"lnmpair:tree|snake":"A detour in steady growth. The Tree and the Snake suggest a complication, or a rival, around something you are building slowly, such as a coworker who muddies a long project. Tip: stay rooted, watch for crooked dealings and take the honest route.",
"lnmpair:tree|coffin":"A natural pause. The Tree and the Coffin read as the end of one growth phase, a rest season or a chapter closing so new roots can form. Finishing a long routine is one example. Tip: let it close fully before planting anything new.",
"lnmpair:tree|bouquet":"Appreciation for steady effort. The Tree and the Bouquet suggest a gift, a kind compliment or pleasure that follows patient work, like friends noticing how much calmer you look. Tip: accept the thanks instead of brushing it aside.",
"lnmpair:tree|scythe":"A clean cut that helps something grow. The Tree and the Scythe point to a quick decision, such as pruning a commitment or dropping a habit that drains you. Tip: cut once and cleanly, then give the space a season to fill.",
"lnmpair:tree|whip":"Effort repeated until it takes hold. The Tree and the Whip describe either friction, like arguing over the same topic again, or steady training such as a regular exercise routine. Tip: choose practice over quarrel and keep the rhythm going.",
"lnmpair:tree|birds":"Chatter around slow growth. The Tree and the Birds suggest lively conversation, a little nervous energy or two people sharing a plan, like friends walking together each morning. Tip: talk it through, but breathe between phone calls.",
"lnmpair:tree|child":"A tender new shoot. The Tree and the Child describe a fresh start in something living: a small habit, a modest project, a simple trust. Think of the first week of a new routine. Tip: be gentle and do not demand fruit yet.",
"lnmpair:tree|fox":"Shrewd, self-interested growth. The Tree and the Fox suggest work that needs cleverness, or someone angling for advantage near something you are nurturing, like a project where credit matters. Tip: stay observant and keep your own records.",
"lnmpair:tree|bear":"A strong trunk with a protector nearby. The Tree and the Bear point to resilience, plentiful resources and a steady, protective person, perhaps a mentor or relative. Tip: lean on that support and offer some back.",
"lnmpair:tree|stars":"Hope with a clear direction. The Tree and the Stars suggest guided, healthy growth and a plan that feels aligned, like choosing a path after a long search. Tip: write the plan down and follow the next small step.",
"lnmpair:tree|stork":"Growth through change. The Tree and the Stork suggest an improvement, a move or a shift that lifts general wellbeing, such as a change of scenery that leaves you feeling lighter. Tip: change one thing at a time so your roots can follow.",
"lnmpair:tree|dog":"A loyal friend beside slow growth. The Tree and the Dog suggest trust and someone dependable who stays through long seasons, like the friend who checks on your progress each week. Tip: return that loyalty in kind.",
"lnmpair:tree|tower":"Growth inside an institution, or in solitude. The Tree and the Tower point to a school, a large organisation or a time alone that lets you develop. A long programme at a big company fits. Tip: respect the rules while guarding your own space.",
"lnmpair:tree|garden":"Growth in public. The Tree and the Garden suggest being seen, gatherings and a social circle that helps you thrive, like a community group that welcomes you. Tip: show up regularly, since belonging grows slowly.",
"lnmpair:tree|mountain":"A slow climb. The Tree and the Mountain point to an obstacle that delays growth and asks for steady patience, like a goal that takes longer than hoped. Tip: pace yourself, rest often and keep your roots deep.",
"lnmpair:tree|crossroads":"Branches pointing different ways. The Tree and the Crossroads describe a choice between paths for long-term growth, such as two offers to learn something new. Tip: pick the option that suits your roots, not the loudest one.",
"lnmpair:tree|mice":"Small drains on steady growth. The Tree and the Mice suggest worry, or little losses wearing at your energy, like lost sleep over a minor task. Tip: tidy the small things and treat rest as a deliberate practice.",
"lnmpair:tree|heart":"Growth fed by affection. The Tree and the Heart suggest love that helps you feel well and rooted, such as time with someone who puts you at ease. Tip: nurture the warmth daily, in small and ordinary ways.",
"lnmpair:tree|ring":"A long cycle or lasting agreement. The Tree and the Ring suggest a commitment that deepens over years, the way trunks add rings, like a lasting partnership or contract. Tip: honour the cycle and renew it on purpose.",
"lnmpair:tree|book":"Quiet growth through study. The Tree and the Book suggest learning, or something not yet known about your direction or wellbeing, like a course taken slowly. Tip: keep notes, and ask for information before assuming.",
"lnmpair:tree|letter":"Growth announced on paper. The Tree and the Letter suggest news, a document or writing that supports something long-term, like an acceptance note or a progress report. Tip: reply thoughtfully, keep a copy for your records, and give the news a day to settle.",
"lnmpair:tree|man":"Someone in a phase of steady growth. The Tree and the Man card describe the person asking, or a significant person, who is putting down roots and building patiently. Tip: ask what support they need, and give it without hurry.",
"lnmpair:tree|woman":"A person growing at their own pace. The Tree and the Woman card describe the person asking, or a significant person, gaining stability and presence over time. Think of someone finally settled in a role. Tip: encourage the slow progress.",
"lnmpair:tree|lily":"Mature calm. The Tree and the Lily suggest peaceful, balanced growth and the wisdom of age, like an elder who seems entirely at ease. Tip: slow your pace to match, skip the rush to results, and let things unfold in their own time.",
"lnmpair:tree|sun":"Vitality and good energy. The Tree and the Sun suggest warmth, success and strong general wellbeing, a bright stretch where steady effort shows. Tip: use the energy for the task that matters most, but protect your rest so the glow lasts.",
"lnmpair:tree|moon":"Slow growth that gets noticed. Read the Tree and the Moon as steady wellbeing paired with a feeling of being recognised, or an intuition that has deep roots. In a question about work, it may be a quiet reputation building year by year. Tip: trust the gut sense that has proven itself over time.",
"lnmpair:tree|key":"A sturdy answer. The Tree and the Key together suggest that a solution grows from patience and good foundations, and that things open up once you stay with the process. Think of the habit that finally clicks after months of practice. Tip: do not hurry the lock, the key fits already.",
"lnmpair:tree|fish":"Money that behaves like a garden. The Tree and the Fish describe income or business that grows steadily, with a healthy flow instead of sudden spikes. A small shop with loyal regulars is a fair picture. Tip: look at what is rooted and sustainable before chasing anything fast or flashy.",
"lnmpair:tree|anchor":"Deep roots, firm footing. Together the Tree and the Anchor speak of lasting stability and a life built to hold through weather. It can point to a home, a routine or a bond that has proven itself over years. Tip: tend what already holds you in place, and give it time.",
"lnmpair:tree|cross":"Growth that asks something of you. The Tree and the Cross can show a long season of responsibility that is also shaping you, like caring for family while building a skill. The weight has meaning. Tip: pace yourself, and ask what this duty is helping to grow in you.",
"lnmpair:clouds|snake":"A murky detour. Read the Clouds and the Snake as confusion around a complication, where it is hard to tell who is being straight with you or which route is truly shorter. Example: mixed signals from a rival at work. Tip: wait for facts before choosing a side.",
"lnmpair:clouds|coffin":"A foggy pause. The Clouds and the Coffin suggest an ending that arrives wrapped in uncertainty, or a stretch where nothing can move until the haze lifts. You may be unsure whether a project is finished. Tip: allow the quiet, and do not force a verdict before it is ready.",
"lnmpair:clouds|bouquet":"A kind gesture in a muddled week. The Clouds and the Bouquet show appreciation that cuts through doubt, or a pleasant surprise that is slightly hard to read. Maybe a gift arrives and you wonder what it means. Tip: accept the warmth first, and sort out the questions afterward.",
"lnmpair:clouds|scythe":"A clean cut through the fog. Here the Clouds and the Scythe point to a quick decision made while the picture is still unclear, which can feel brave or rash. Example: cancelling a plan on impulse. Tip: sharpen one question before swinging, so the cut serves you.",
"lnmpair:clouds|whip":"Arguments going in circles. The Clouds and the Whip describe repeated friction where neither side sees the other clearly, and effort keeps being spent without progress. Think of the same disagreement at the dinner table. Tip: stop, restate what you actually heard, and only then respond.",
"lnmpair:clouds|birds":"Anxious chatter. The Clouds and the Birds show a lot of talk and nerves, with rumours or overthinking filling the gaps. A group chat that spirals is a good example. Tip: step away from the noise, speak to one person directly, and let the facts come first.",
"lnmpair:clouds|child":"A hesitant beginning. Read the Clouds and the Child as a new start that feels uncertain, or a small matter you are worrying about more than it deserves. It may be a first day somewhere unfamiliar. Tip: take the first step small and keep it light.",
"lnmpair:clouds|fox":"Someone may not be showing the full picture. The Clouds and the Fox suggest murky motives at work or a plan that is not as simple as it looks. Example: a vague offer with unclear terms. Tip: ask plain questions, write the answers down, and trust careful observation.",
"lnmpair:clouds|bear":"Strength dimmed by doubt. The Clouds and the Bear can show a capable person or solid resource that is temporarily hard to rely on, or a protector who seems distracted. Maybe a mentor is hard to reach. Tip: remember what you already have, even when the view is poor.",
"lnmpair:clouds|stars":"Clarity on the way. The Clouds and the Stars show hope pushing through a period of doubt, like the sky clearing after an overcast day. A confusing situation starts to make sense. Tip: keep following the guidance you do have, and give the last of the fog time to lift.",
"lnmpair:clouds|stork":"Change in unsettled weather. The Clouds and the Stork point to a move or an improvement that comes with questions attached, such as a new place where little feels familiar yet. Tip: expect a short adjustment period, and ask for the details you need before leaning in.",
"lnmpair:clouds|dog":"A trusted friend in a hazy moment. The Clouds and the Dog suggest loyalty that holds steady while you feel unsure, or a doubt about whether someone close is reliable. Example: wondering if a friend meant what they said. Tip: ask them directly, since the bond is probably sound.",
"lnmpair:clouds|tower":"Opaque systems. Read the Clouds and the Tower as confusion around an institution, an official process or a period of isolation. Think of a form that nobody can explain, or a long wait on a decision. Tip: keep records, request clear answers in writing, and be patient with the process.",
"lnmpair:clouds|garden":"An awkward social scene. The Clouds and the Garden show uncertainty in public or among groups, like a gathering where you cannot read the room. Maybe a party full of unfamiliar faces. Tip: observe first, then join one conversation, and do not judge the whole crowd too soon.",
"lnmpair:clouds|mountain":"A blocked road in poor visibility. The Clouds and the Mountain describe an obstacle made harder by not knowing exactly what it is. Example: a delay with no clear reason given. Tip: gather information about the actual size of the problem before you decide how to climb it.",
"lnmpair:clouds|crossroads":"A choice without a clear view. The Clouds and the Crossroads show options that are hard to compare, because the information is incomplete. Picture two job offers with vague details. Tip: delay the decision if you can, and list what you still need to know about each path.",
"lnmpair:clouds|mice":"Small worries wearing you down. The Clouds and the Mice suggest doubt feeding on itself, where little losses or nagging thoughts pile up without a clear cause. Example: lying awake over a minor task. Tip: write the worry down, name one concrete step, and take it today.",
"lnmpair:clouds|heart":"Feelings that are hard to read. Read the Clouds and the Heart as affection mixed with uncertainty, perhaps about where a bond stands or what someone feels. Maybe a warm message arrives and its tone is ambiguous. Tip: say what you feel plainly and invite the same.",
"lnmpair:clouds|ring":"An agreement with fine print. The Clouds and the Ring point to a commitment, contract or cycle that is not fully clear yet. Example: an arrangement where the terms keep shifting. Tip: pause before you sign anything, ask for the terms in plain words, and take time to read them.",
"lnmpair:clouds|book":"A secret within a secret. The Clouds and the Book show hidden information that is also hard to interpret, or study that feels like trying to read in bad light. A dense subject you have just started is a fair example. Tip: seek a good teacher and go step by step.",
"lnmpair:clouds|letter":"A message that does not settle anything. The Clouds and the Letter suggest unclear writing, a delayed reply or a document that raises more questions than answers. Think of an email with an ambiguous tone. Tip: ask for clarification instead of guessing, and keep your own reply simple.",
"lnmpair:clouds|man":"The person asking, or someone significant, is in a fog. Read the Clouds and the Man card as doubt or confusion around that person, or around how they see themselves right now. They may be unsure about a next step. Tip: patience and plain questions help more than pressure.",
"lnmpair:clouds|woman":"The person asking, or someone significant, is in a fog. Read the Clouds and the Woman card as doubt or mixed feelings around that person, or about how they are being seen. They may be waiting for clarity. Tip: listen carefully, and offer steady, simple support.",
"lnmpair:clouds|lily":"Calm underneath the haze. The Clouds and the Lily suggest that peace and maturity are available even while things remain unclear, as in an older relative who stays composed in a muddle. Tip: lean on your own steadiness, and let the situation settle without forcing answers.",
"lnmpair:clouds|sun":"Brightness breaking through. Read the Clouds and the Sun as a doubtful period giving way to energy and success, like a grey morning turning into a warm afternoon. A problem starts to look manageable. Tip: act on the clearer view while it lasts, and do not dwell on the earlier gloom.",
"lnmpair:clouds|moon":"Mood and intuition under a haze. The Clouds and the Moon point to feelings that are hard to name, or recognition that is slow to come. Example: a sense that something is off without any proof. Tip: keep a short journal and wait for the pattern to show itself.",
"lnmpair:clouds|key":"A solution that needs a clearer look. The Clouds and the Key suggest that an answer exists but is not yet visible, or that certainty is near though doubt lingers. Maybe a puzzle where one clue is missing. Tip: ask the one question that would settle it.",
"lnmpair:clouds|fish":"Unclear finances or business flow. Read the Clouds and the Fish as uncertainty around money matters, like a shop whose numbers are hard to interpret. It may simply be a temporary lull. Tip: review the records calmly, ask a qualified professional if needed, and avoid reacting to rumours.",
"lnmpair:clouds|anchor":"Staying put in a mist. The Clouds and the Anchor show a need for steadiness while the long-term picture is unclear. Example: keeping routines going during a confusing season. Tip: hold to the habits and values that keep you steady, and let the horizon clear before changing course.",
"lnmpair:clouds|cross":"A heavy duty that is hard to understand. The Clouds and the Cross describe a burden that feels meaningless or confusing, or a doubt about what you owe someone. Tip: break the load into pieces, ask what is truly yours to carry, and share it where you can.",
"lnmpair:snake|coffin":"A complication that closes a door. The Snake and the Coffin suggest an ending reached by a long, winding road, or a rival situation finally going quiet. Example: a drawn-out dispute that stops by itself. Tip: let the matter rest, and do not reopen it just to win.",
"lnmpair:snake|bouquet":"A charming surface with a twist. Read the Snake and the Bouquet as a pleasant gesture that may carry a hidden angle, or a gift that comes by an indirect route. Compliments from a rival are one example. Tip: enjoy the kindness, and keep your eyes open.",
"lnmpair:snake|scythe":"A sharp turn in a twisty plot. The Snake and the Scythe point to a sudden decision that cuts through a tangled situation, such as walking away from a murky deal. It can feel abrupt but clean. Tip: be sure the cut removes the knot and not the good parts.",
"lnmpair:snake|whip":"Friction with a rival, repeated. The Snake and the Whip suggest an ongoing tangle where conflict keeps resurfacing through indirect means, like a petty feud in a shared workspace. Tip: stop replaying the argument, choose one calm boundary, and hold to it.",
"lnmpair:snake|birds":"Gossip and winding talk. Read the Snake and the Birds as chatter with an angle, where a conversation circles around the real point and nerves run high. Think of a group call full of hints. Tip: ask for a direct statement, and share only what you are comfortable seeing repeated.",
"lnmpair:snake|child":"A tangled start. The Snake and the Child describe a new project, habit or friendship that hits a snag in its first weeks. Think of a new hire whose paperwork keeps getting lost. Go slowly, ask for clear instructions, and do not judge the whole thing by its awkward beginning.",
"lnmpair:snake|fox":"A sly workplace angle. The Snake and the Fox suggest someone is playing office politics, or a job task is more roundabout than it looks. Maybe a coworker takes credit in a meeting. Keep your own notes, stay polite, and check the details before you sign off on anything.",
"lnmpair:snake|bear":"Complication met by strength. The Snake and the Bear show a twisty problem that a steady ally or solid reserves can absorb. A relative with experience might untangle the paperwork you have been dreading. Lean on what you have built up, and accept real help when it is offered.",
"lnmpair:snake|stars":"A winding road with a light at the end. The Snake and the Stars point to a detour that still leads somewhere clear. Perhaps a confusing course change lets you see your goal better. Write down what you actually want, then follow the thread one turn at a time.",
"lnmpair:snake|stork":"Change that arrives by a roundabout route. Pairing the Snake with the Stork suggests a move or improvement that takes more steps than hoped. Maybe a new apartment needs three rounds of forms. Expect the long way, keep your documents in order, and the shift does settle.",
"lnmpair:snake|dog":"A loyal friend caught in something knotty, or a trust that gets tested. The Snake and the Dog ask whether someone close is quite as open as they seem. Maybe a pal dodges a direct question. Ask kindly and plainly, and let their answer, not a hunch, guide you.",
"lnmpair:snake|tower":"A twisting path through an institution. The Snake and the Tower describe red tape, a slow bureaucracy or an authority figure who is hard to read. Picture waiting on hold with a large office. Get names, keep copies, and be patient but persistent with each department.",
"lnmpair:snake|garden":"Tangles in the social scene. Read together, the Snake and the Garden show gossip, an awkward guest list or a rival at a gathering. Maybe two friend groups clash at a party. Stay friendly, avoid taking sides in public, and talk to the person directly afterward.",
"lnmpair:snake|mountain":"A hard obstacle with extra twists. The Snake and the Mountain mark a delay that also comes with confusion, such as a stalled application that keeps needing new forms. Rest, reroute and look for a side path. Brute force alone rarely helps here.",
"lnmpair:snake|crossroads":"Options that are harder to read than they look. The Snake and the Crossroads warn that one path may carry a hidden catch, like a job offer with vague terms. Ask pointed questions before choosing. Compare the fine print, then pick the route you can explain out loud.",
"lnmpair:snake|mice":"Small leaks that add up. The Snake and the Mice describe worry gnawing at a situation already complicated, such as a tight month where hidden fees keep appearing. Trace each leak to its source and fix the easiest one first. Naming the worry shrinks it.",
"lnmpair:snake|heart":"A knotted love story. The Snake and the Heart point to jealousy, mixed signals or a third party in matters of affection. Maybe someone is warm one day and distant the next. Say what you feel in plain words, and notice whether actions match what is said.",
"lnmpair:snake|ring":"A commitment with complicated terms. The Snake and the Ring suggest a contract, partnership or agreement where the details are tricky. Maybe a lease has clauses nobody explained. Read every line, ask for changes in writing, and take your time before you put your name down.",
"lnmpair:snake|book":"A hidden layer to something you are studying. The Snake and the Book show secrets, a confusing subject or information that someone withholds. Maybe a manual is missing its key chapter. Dig for a second source, ask an expert, and keep your notes private until you understand.",
"lnmpair:snake|letter":"A message with a twist. The Snake and the Letter hint at an email, text or document that is vague, loaded or easy to misread. Maybe a reply sounds polite but says little. Read it twice, ask a clarifying question, and keep a copy before you answer.",
"lnmpair:snake|man":"The Man card meets a complication. The Snake beside it suggests the person asking, or someone important to them, is caught in a tricky spot or dealing with a rival. Step back, list who truly benefits, and choose your next move calmly rather than reacting in the moment.",
"lnmpair:snake|woman":"The Woman card meets a complication. The Snake beside it suggests the person asking, or someone significant to them, is navigating a knotty situation or an envious competitor. Pause, sort fact from rumor, and respond with clear words instead of a quick defense.",
"lnmpair:snake|lily":"Calm tested by a snag. The Snake and the Lily describe a peaceful season that runs into a prickly issue, like a quiet household disturbed by an old grievance. Handle it with patience and maturity. A gentle, honest talk usually does more than silence.",
"lnmpair:snake|sun":"Success that comes with a catch. The Snake and the Sun show a bright result arriving through an unexpected detour, or a rival who envies your progress. Maybe a promotion brings office jealousy. Enjoy the win, stay gracious, and watch for anyone who quietly undermines it.",
"lnmpair:snake|moon":"Recognition that gets complicated. The Snake and the Moon describe feelings, reputation or intuition tangled with some hidden motive. Maybe praise comes with strings attached. Trust your gut reaction, ask what is really being offered, and keep your emotional boundaries clear.",
"lnmpair:snake|key":"A clever fix for a knotty problem. The Snake and the Key show an answer hiding inside the complication. Maybe a confusing billing error clears up after one precise phone call. Look for the single detail that unlocks the rest, and be willing to ask the right person.",
"lnmpair:snake|fish":"Money matters that run crooked. The Snake and the Fish describe a business deal or cash flow with a twist, such as a client who pays late and keeps changing terms. Keep clean records, confirm agreements in writing, and favor transparent partners over charming ones.",
"lnmpair:snake|anchor":"Staying steady through a winding stretch. The Snake and the Anchor suggest a long-term goal with plenty of detours, like finishing a degree around a busy job. Hold your course, break the road into small checkpoints, and trust that persistence outlasts the tangles.",
"lnmpair:snake|cross":"A heavy duty with extra knots. The Snake and the Cross mark a burden that is also confusing, perhaps caring for a family matter full of old disagreements. Ask what is truly yours to carry, share the load where you can, and look for the meaning in it.",
"lnmpair:coffin|bouquet":"A graceful goodbye. The Coffin and the Bouquet show an ending marked by kindness, like a farewell party for a colleague who is leaving. Something closes, yet appreciation remains. Accept the thanks being offered, and let the gentle moment be part of the finish.",
"lnmpair:coffin|scythe":"A clean, decisive cut. Reading the Coffin with the Scythe, an ending arrives quickly and without much negotiation, such as a contract that simply stops. It can sting, yet it clears space. Do not drag out the paperwork, and give yourself a day to absorb it.",
"lnmpair:coffin|whip":"A fight that has run its course. The Coffin and the Whip describe repeated arguing finally reaching a stopping point, or the need to end a draining routine. Maybe the same quarrel keeps resurfacing at dinner. Call a pause, let things cool, and choose a calmer way to talk.",
"lnmpair:coffin|birds":"Quiet after the chatter. The Coffin and the Birds point to talk that fades, or a nervous pair of voices falling silent. Maybe a group chat goes still after a long debate. Use the stillness to rest your nerves before the next conversation begins.",
"lnmpair:coffin|child":"The close of a first chapter. The Coffin and the Child show something small ending before it grows, like a trial project that does not continue. It is a pause, not a verdict on you. Take what you learned and keep the door open for a fresh try.",
"lnmpair:coffin|fox":"A work chapter closing. The Coffin and the Fox suggest a job, scheme or work arrangement reaching its end, or a cunning plan that fizzles out. Maybe a side hustle runs its course. Tidy your files, collect your references, and think about what skills travel with you.",
"lnmpair:coffin|bear":"Strength gone quiet. The Coffin and the Bear describe a protector or a store of resources stepping back, such as a mentor retiring or savings put on hold. Rest is part of the cycle. List what you still hold and rebuild at a slower pace.",
"lnmpair:coffin|stars":"Hope after a closing. The Coffin and the Stars read as clarity arriving once something finishes, like seeing your next goal only after a long project ends. Let the old one rest. Sit with a quiet evening, then write down the direction that feels clear.",
"lnmpair:coffin|stork":"Endings that make room for moves. The Coffin and the Stork speak of a chapter closing so something better can arrive, such as leaving a cramped apartment for a brighter one. There may be a gap in between. Pack patiently and trust the transition to settle.",
"lnmpair:coffin|dog":"A friendship pausing. The Coffin and the Dog suggest a loyal bond going quiet, or a trusted ally stepping out for a while. Maybe a longtime friend moves far away. Send a note anyway. A pause is not always a break, and good loyalty often waits.",
"lnmpair:coffin|tower":"An institution closing its doors, or a solitary phase ending. The Coffin and the Tower might show a school year concluding, a company restructuring or a long stretch alone wrapping up. Ask what comes next, request clear steps from the authorities involved, and give yourself time.",
"lnmpair:coffin|garden":"The party winds down. The Coffin and the Garden describe a social season ending, a group disbanding or a gathering calling it a night. Maybe a club holds its final meeting. Say your thanks, keep a few contacts, and look for new company when you feel ready.",
"lnmpair:coffin|mountain":"A long delay reaching its limit. The Coffin and the Mountain show a stalled situation finally closing, whether by giving up the climb or by finishing it. Maybe a drawn-out dispute is dropped. Decide whether to rest at this point or find another route, and be honest about what you can carry.",
"lnmpair:coffin|crossroads":"A closed path makes the choice. The Coffin and the Crossroads suggest one option is ending, which narrows your decision. Maybe a program you considered is no longer offered. Grieve it briefly, then look carefully at the roads that remain open to you.",
"lnmpair:coffin|mice":"A slow wearing down reaching its end. The Coffin and the Mice show worry or a gradual loss finally stopping, such as a subscription you quit after months of small drains. Relief can follow the pause. Check where else small leaks hide and close them now.",
"lnmpair:coffin|heart":"A tender stillness. The Coffin and the Heart describe a love chapter closing or a relationship going quiet to rest, like a long-distance bond pausing for the season. It may ache. Be gentle with yourself, express what you feel honestly, and let affection find its natural shape again.",
"lnmpair:coffin|ring":"A promise reaches its natural close. The Coffin and the Ring point to a contract that runs out, a commitment being set down, or a cycle that has simply finished. Example: a lease ending, or an agreement nobody renews. Tip: mark the close deliberately, so the next chapter starts clean.",
"lnmpair:coffin|book":"Something stays shut. The Coffin and the Book suggest a closed file, a paused course, or a secret that is better left sealed for now. Example: a project put on a shelf while you gather facts. Tip: pausing to learn more is not failure, so set a date to reopen it.",
"lnmpair:coffin|letter":"Words come to a stop. The Coffin and the Letter can mean a final message, a document that closes a matter, or a long silence in writing. Example: the last email in a thread, or a notice you have been avoiding. Tip: send or file it, then let the matter rest.",
"lnmpair:coffin|man":"The Man card meets a pause. Read this pair as the person asking, or someone important to them, in a still or finished phase: tired of an old role, or ready to close a chapter. Example: stepping back from a duty. Tip: rest first, and decide what comes next afterward.",
"lnmpair:coffin|woman":"The Woman card meets a pause. Here the person this card stands for is winding something down, ending a role, or going quiet for a while. Example: a mentor stepping away from a project. Tip: respect the quiet, and do not rush to fill the gap it leaves.",
"lnmpair:coffin|lily":"Calm follows a close. The Coffin and the Lily describe a quiet ending, handled with maturity: a retirement, a settled dispute, a house that finally goes still. Example: a long job wrapping up without drama. Tip: let the peace be enough, and resist stirring things up.",
"lnmpair:coffin|sun":"Light after a closing. The Coffin and the Sun read as an ending that clears the air, or energy returning once something heavy is put down. Example: finishing a draining task and feeling the day open up. Tip: celebrate the finish, however small, before moving on.",
"lnmpair:coffin|moon":"Feelings go quiet. The Coffin and the Moon point to a closed chapter that still echoes inside, or recognition that fades from view. Example: missing a role you chose to leave. Tip: give the mood a little time and a plain name, and it usually settles.",
"lnmpair:coffin|key":"An ending that opens a way. The Coffin and the Key suggest a closed door that turns out to be the answer, a clear final decision, or the end of a puzzle. Example: finally admitting a plan is done. Tip: accept the closure, then look for the opening it made.",
"lnmpair:coffin|fish":"Flow slows down. The Coffin and the Fish describe a business pause, a stalled stream of work, or an income source winding down. Example: a slow season for a small shop. Tip: use the lull to tidy the books and rethink where your effort goes.",
"lnmpair:coffin|anchor":"Stability meets an ending. The Coffin and the Anchor can mean a long commitment finally closing, or a steady habit holding firm through a pause. Example: leaving a job held for years. Tip: honor what lasted, and choose the next foundation with care.",
"lnmpair:coffin|cross":"A heavy load is being set down. The Coffin and the Cross suggest a burden reaching its end, or a duty finally released. Example: a family obligation winding down after years. Tip: let the relief arrive without guilt, and keep the meaning you found in carrying it.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|scythe":"A pleasant surprise cuts both ways. The Bouquet and the Scythe point to a gift that arrives suddenly, or a quick decision made about something nice. Example: an offer that needs an answer today. Tip: enjoy it, but take one calm breath before replying.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|whip":"Pleasure meets friction. The Bouquet and the Whip suggest a celebration with a squabble inside it, or a kind gesture that gets argued over. Example: a family dinner where old topics resurface. Tip: keep the gift separate from the quarrel, and thank people anyway.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|birds":"Chatty good cheer. The Bouquet and the Birds describe pleasant talk, compliments traded between two people, or nervous excitement before a happy event. Example: two friends buzzing about a shared plan. Tip: say the kind thing aloud, since small praise travels far.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|child":"A small, sweet beginning. The Bouquet and the Child point to a fresh pleasure, an innocent kindness, or a modest new venture that feels light. Example: a first lesson in something you have always wanted to try. Tip: stay playful, and do not weigh it down yet.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|fox":"A gift with an angle. The Bouquet and the Fox warn that a kind gesture may carry a motive, or that charm is being used at work. Example: flattery just before a request. Tip: accept graciously, but ask yourself what is being asked in return.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|bear":"Generous support. The Bouquet and the Bear describe a protector who gives freely, or resources shared warmly. Example: a mentor or relative offering real help and asking little. Tip: accept it with thanks, and offer something back when you can.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|stars":"Hope with a pleasant face. The Bouquet and the Stars suggest encouragement that rings true, a clear sense of direction, or praise that points the way. Example: feedback that confirms a path you half suspected. Tip: write it down for the harder days.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|stork":"A change for the better. The Bouquet and the Stork point to a welcome move, a pleasant shift in circumstances, or an upgrade you did not chase. Example: a new setting that feels right within a week. Tip: say yes to the improvement, and loosen your grip on the old routine.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|dog":"Kindness from a loyal friend. The Bouquet and the Dog describe trusted company, a thoughtful gesture from someone dependable, or loyalty that is quietly appreciated. Example: a friend who shows up with soup and asks nothing. Tip: tell that friend plainly that it mattered.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|tower":"A pleasure behind walls. The Bouquet and the Tower suggest a gift from an institution, kindness from an authority, or a welcome moment of solitude. Example: a bank or school treating you better than expected. Tip: enjoy it, and keep your own space too.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|garden":"A happy gathering. The Bouquet and the Garden point to a party, a warm public moment, or appreciation from a wider circle. Example: a neighborhood event where people greet you by name. Tip: show up, since goodwill grows when it is shared in person.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|mountain":"A nice thing is slow to arrive. The Bouquet and the Mountain suggest a pleasure delayed, a gift stuck behind an obstacle, or joy that takes effort. Example: a trip postponed more than once. Tip: stay patient, and enjoy the small pleasures on the way up.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|crossroads":"Two pleasant options. The Bouquet and the Crossroads describe a choice where both paths hold something good, or invitations competing for the same weekend. Example: two offers that each flatter you. Tip: pick by what feels warm, not by what looks impressive.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|mice":"A gift slowly wearing thin. The Bouquet and the Mice suggest a pleasure nibbled by worry, or goodwill eroding through small neglect. Example: a lovely plan spoiled by a dozen tiny annoyances. Tip: deal with the small irritations early, before they add up.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|heart":"Warm affection, openly shown. The Bouquet and the Heart point to tenderness, a romantic gesture, or simple fondness between people. Example: a handwritten note left where it will be found. Tip: do not save the sweet words for special occasions.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|ring":"A pleasant bond. The Bouquet and the Ring describe an agreement that feels good, a commitment celebrated, or a friendly partnership renewed. Example: signing something with people you like. Tip: read the terms anyway, and keep the happy mood for after.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|book":"A quiet delight in learning. The Bouquet and the Book suggest a gift of knowledge, a pleasant secret, or study that feels like a treat. Example: a surprise book from someone who knows your taste. Tip: set aside an hour with no screen and enjoy it.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|letter":"Good words arrive. The Bouquet and the Letter point to a kind message, an invitation, or a warm note that lifts the day. Example: a thank-you from someone you had nearly forgotten helping. Tip: answer promptly while the warmth is fresh.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|man":"Charm and courtesy. The Bouquet and the Man card describe the person asking, or someone significant, in a gracious mood: generous, appreciated, easy to be around. Example: a pleasant meeting that goes better than planned. Tip: be gracious back and let things unfold.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|woman":"Grace and appreciation. The Bouquet and the Woman card describe the person this card stands for being warm, admired, or receiving a kindness. Example: a colleague praised in front of the team. Tip: share the credit, and the good feeling spreads.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|lily":"Gentle harmony. The Bouquet and the Lily suggest a peaceful pleasure, mature affection, or a calm home with something lovely in it. Example: a quiet evening that needs no entertainment. Tip: protect that calm, and decline the extra obligation.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|sun":"Bright and generous. The Bouquet and the Sun describe a successful, happy stretch, warm recognition, and energy that others enjoy. Example: a presentation that lands well and gets thanked. Tip: spend the good mood on others, not only on yourself.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|moon":"Appreciation felt inside. The Bouquet and the Moon point to recognition that touches the emotions, or a tender mood that wants a kind word. Example: a compliment that stays with you all evening. Tip: trust the feeling, and let yourself be thanked.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|key":"A pleasant answer. The Bouquet and the Key suggest a solution that arrives gracefully, or a gift that opens a door. Example: a friendly introduction that settles a stuck situation. Tip: say thank you, and use the opening while it is still fresh.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|fish":"Pleasant flow of resources. The Bouquet and the Fish describe business that feels good, goodwill that helps trade, or generous exchange. Example: a client who pays on time and sends a kind note. Tip: keep the relationship warm, since goodwill is its own asset.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|anchor":"Lasting appreciation. The Bouquet and the Anchor suggest a pleasure that endures, steady gratitude, or a long-term bond that still has charm. Example: a friendship decades old that still trades small gifts. Tip: keep the rituals that hold it together.",
"lnmpair:bouquet|cross":"Meaning inside a duty. The Bouquet and the Cross describe a burden softened by thanks, or a task done for love. Example: caring for a relative and finally hearing real appreciation. Tip: let yourself receive the thanks, and ask for help too.",
"lnmpair:scythe|whip":"Sharp words, repeated. The Scythe and the Whip point to a quick, cutting argument, or a decisive effort that must be done again and again. Example: a heated exchange that keeps resurfacing. Tip: pause before you speak, and cut only what truly needs cutting.",
"lnmpair:scythe|birds":"A quick, clean cut in a conversation. The Scythe and the Birds can point to a nervous talk that ends abruptly, or two people deciding something in a single phone call. Think of a chat that finally stops circling. Tip: say the one sentence that matters before the line goes quiet.",
"lnmpair:scythe|child":"A small fresh start made by a sharp decision. The Scythe and the Child suggest clearing away the old habit so something new and modest can begin, like deleting a stale routine and trying a simple one. Tip: keep the new thing small and let it grow at its own pace.",
"lnmpair:scythe|fox":"Cutting through cunning, or trimming a work arrangement that does not add up. The Scythe and the Fox ask you to look at who benefits, then act decisively, for example dropping a task that was always quietly self-serving. Tip: check the facts first, then cut once.",
"lnmpair:scythe|bear":"Strength used with precision. The Scythe and the Bear describe a powerful person or resource applied to one clear cut, such as a firm boss making a call, or you spending your energy only where it counts. Tip: use your weight sparingly and aim it well.",
"lnmpair:scythe|stars":"Clarity arrives through a clean break. The Scythe and the Stars suggest that letting go of one muddy option makes the real direction visible, like closing extra browser tabs and finally seeing the plan. Tip: trust the clear idea that remains afterward.",
"lnmpair:scythe|stork":"A sharp edge in the middle of a change. The Scythe and the Stork point to a move or improvement that begins with a decisive step, like giving notice on a lease or a habit. Tip: finish the cut before you start packing the new thing.",
"lnmpair:scythe|dog":"A loyal friend and a sudden decision. The Scythe and the Dog can mean a blunt but honest word between trusted people, or a quick call to step back from a friendship that stopped being mutual. Tip: be direct and kind in the same breath.",
"lnmpair:scythe|tower":"A decisive move involving an institution or a solitary stretch. The Scythe and the Tower might be a firm answer from an office, a school or an authority, or a choice to step away alone for a while. Tip: read the official wording twice before reacting.",
"lnmpair:scythe|garden":"A cut in public or social life. The Scythe and the Garden suggest trimming a guest list, leaving a group chat, or ending a crowded commitment so calmer gatherings fit. Tip: choose the gatherings that actually feed you, and prune the rest politely.",
"lnmpair:scythe|mountain":"A hard decision against a big obstacle. The Scythe and the Mountain say the way through may be one firm cut rather than slow pushing, like dropping an impossible part of a project. Tip: stop hauling what cannot be carried and keep the route simple.",
"lnmpair:scythe|crossroads":"A fork that asks for a clean choice. The Scythe and the Crossroads mean deciding between options and closing the other path properly, such as picking one offer and telling the rest no. Tip: choose, then commit without keeping a back door open.",
"lnmpair:scythe|mice":"Worry gets cut off at the root. The Scythe and the Mice suggest ending the slow leak, like cancelling the small subscriptions that nag or finally settling a lingering concern. Tip: name the one thing gnawing at you and deal with that item first.",
"lnmpair:scythe|heart":"A decisive moment in matters of affection. The Scythe and the Heart can mean an honest cut to old hurt, or a bold, clear statement of feeling after long hesitation. Tip: say it plainly and gently, and let warmth carry the sharpness.",
"lnmpair:scythe|ring":"A cycle or agreement reaches a firm edge. The Scythe and the Ring can point to a contract ending, terms rewritten, or a commitment redefined, like an old arrangement closed and a cleaner one drawn up. Tip: put the new terms in writing.",
"lnmpair:scythe|book":"A secret or a subject is cut open. The Scythe and the Book suggest sudden knowledge, a course dropped, or a mystery resolved by one clear fact, like finally reading the fine print. Tip: learn what is actually written before deciding.",
"lnmpair:scythe|letter":"A short, decisive message. The Scythe and the Letter point to a blunt email, a resignation note, or a document that changes course, such as a firm reply you have put off. Tip: keep it brief, polite and clear, and let it sit an hour before sending.",
"lnmpair:scythe|man":"The person asking, or someone significant, is in a cutting mood. The Scythe and the Man card suggest a sharp decision made by that person, or a need to trim something from their life. Tip: act on the decision that has already been clear for some time.",
"lnmpair:scythe|woman":"The person asking, or someone significant, is ready to cut something loose. The Scythe and the Woman card describe a firm choice, often about clutter, a role, or a habit. Tip: choose what to release before anyone else chooses for you.",
"lnmpair:scythe|lily":"A calm harvest. The Scythe and the Lily mean a peaceful ending, a mature decision made without drama, like finishing a long project and quietly closing the file. Tip: let the ending be gentle and give yourself time to rest afterward.",
"lnmpair:scythe|sun":"A clean cut that leads to light. The Scythe and the Sun suggest a decisive step bringing relief and energy, like ending a draining routine and feeling lighter by the weekend. Tip: notice the warmth that follows and let it confirm the choice.",
"lnmpair:scythe|moon":"An emotional decision made suddenly. The Scythe and the Moon point to feelings reaching a cutting point, or recognition arriving after an abrupt step. Tip: sleep on the decision once before acting, then follow what your gut still says in the morning.",
"lnmpair:scythe|key":"The decisive answer. The Scythe and the Key suggest one firm action opens the situation, like making the call or signing off on the thing that unlocks everything else. Tip: do the single step you have been circling, because it is likely the whole solution.",
"lnmpair:scythe|fish":"A cut in money matters or the flow of business. The Scythe and the Fish suggest trimming expenses, closing a deal, or redirecting resources, such as ending an unprofitable side task. Tip: look at the numbers calmly before slicing anything.",
"lnmpair:scythe|anchor":"A firm decision about the long term. The Scythe and the Anchor mean cutting loose what does not hold, so something steady can stay, like leaving a shaky arrangement in favor of lasting stability. Tip: keep what has proven reliable.",
"lnmpair:scythe|cross":"A heavy duty is finally trimmed. The Scythe and the Cross suggest putting down a burden through a clear decision, or finding the meaning in a hard chapter that is closing. Tip: hand over what is not yours to carry and keep the lesson.",
"lnmpair:whip|birds":"Repeated chatter. The Whip and the Birds describe arguments that loop, or a nervous exchange of messages going back and forth, like two people rehashing the same point at dinner. Tip: agree to one short, calm talk and stop the replay afterward.",
"lnmpair:whip|child":"Effort on something small and new. The Whip and the Child suggest practising a beginner skill over and over, or a squabble over something minor. Think of daily scales on a new instrument. Tip: keep the practice short and forgive the early mistakes.",
"lnmpair:whip|fox":"Friction at work. The Whip and the Fox point to a tense office dynamic, or exhausting repetition in a job where someone plays angles. Tip: document what is agreed in writing and protect your energy from needless contests.",
"lnmpair:whip|bear":"Strength spent in a struggle. The Whip and the Bear suggest hard physical effort, a tough training routine, or a standoff with a dominant person. Tip: pace yourself the way an athlete does, and rest between rounds.",
"lnmpair:whip|stars":"Effort toward a clear goal. The Whip and the Stars suggest repeated work that pays off through guidance and persistence, like drilling a plan until it becomes second nature. Tip: keep your sights on the aim when the routine feels dull.",
"lnmpair:whip|stork":"A bumpy change. The Whip and the Stork point to a move or improvement that takes repeated effort, like a renovation with several rounds of fixes. Tip: expect a few rough passes and build in spare time.",
"lnmpair:whip|dog":"Strain between loyal people. The Whip and the Dog suggest a friend you keep working at, or a disagreement among trusted allies, such as a recurring row over plans. Tip: remember what you value in them and say so before the argument starts.",
"lnmpair:whip|tower":"Tension with authority or isolation. The Whip and the Tower can mean repeated rounds with an institution, like forms sent back again and again, or pushing against your own walls. Tip: stay formal, patient and thorough with paperwork.",
"lnmpair:whip|garden":"Social friction in public. The Whip and the Garden suggest heated talk at a gathering, or a busy season of events that wears you down. Tip: pick the occasions that matter and leave early from the ones that do not.",
"lnmpair:whip|mountain":"Pushing against something immovable. The Whip and the Mountain describe long, repeated effort against a hard obstacle, like a stubborn problem you keep hitting. Tip: change the angle of attack instead of simply pushing harder.",
"lnmpair:whip|crossroads":"Going back and forth over a choice. The Whip and the Crossroads suggest repeated debate about which path to take, like rewriting the same pros and cons list. Tip: set a deadline for deciding and stick to it.",
"lnmpair:whip|mice":"Worry that keeps gnawing. The Whip and the Mice point to stress made worse by repetition, such as replaying a small mistake over and over at night. Tip: write the worry down once, then do one useful thing about it.",
"lnmpair:whip|heart":"Passion with a sharp edge. The Whip and the Heart suggest affection that includes friction, or lively disagreements between people who care. Think of a couple who debate everything. Tip: keep the arguments about the issue, never about worth.",
"lnmpair:whip|ring":"Strain around a commitment. The Whip and the Ring point to repeated negotiation of an agreement, or a cycle that keeps coming back, like contract terms revised again and again. Tip: pin down the sticking points in one clear session.",
"lnmpair:whip|book":"Hard study. The Whip and the Book suggest repeated effort to learn something difficult, or digging at a secret that does not yield easily, like rereading a dense chapter before an exam. Tip: study in short blocks and test yourself.",
"lnmpair:whip|letter":"Words that keep landing like blows. The Whip and the Letter point to a sharp message, an email thread that flares up again and again, or a draft you keep revising. Tip: state your main point once, calmly, then wait for an answer before writing more.",
"lnmpair:whip|man":"Friction around a particular person, or around you. The Whip and the Man card describe someone locked in repeated effort or a running disagreement, like a coworker who restarts the same argument every week. Tip: pick one issue to settle and let the rest rest.",
"lnmpair:whip|woman":"A person caught in a loop of tension or hard work. The Whip beside the Woman card can describe a running dispute with someone close, or the person this card stands for pushing hard at a task. Tip: pause between rounds, because rest changes the tone.",
"lnmpair:whip|lily":"Calm set against conflict. The Whip and the Lily show an argument cooling down, or the effort it takes to stay gentle with someone who provokes you. Example: a family dinner where you choose patience. Tip: repeat the peaceful move until it sticks.",
"lnmpair:whip|sun":"Energy with a lot of push behind it. The Whip and the Sun read as vigorous, confident effort, a training routine or a project driven with real warmth. The risk is overdoing it. Tip: keep the pace that feels bright, not the one that feels punishing.",
"lnmpair:whip|moon":"Feelings stirred up by repetition. The Whip and the Moon suggest an emotional pattern that keeps replaying, or hard work that goes unnoticed until someone finally says thanks. Example: a task done over and over for little credit. Tip: ask for the recognition you need.",
"lnmpair:whip|key":"Effort that finally opens something. The Whip and the Key suggest that steady, repeated practice is what unlocks the answer, like rehearsing a speech until it clicks. It can also mean a quarrel ends with a clear decision. Tip: keep going one more round.",
"lnmpair:whip|fish":"Hard work tied to business and flow. The Whip and the Fish describe busy, repetitive commercial effort, such as chasing leads all week or reworking a price list. Pressure and income move together here. Tip: set a daily cap so the effort stays steady rather than frantic.",
"lnmpair:whip|anchor":"Endurance under pressure. The Whip and the Anchor suggest holding steady through a long stretch of strain, like finishing a degree while working. Persistence is the strength here, stubbornness the shadow. Tip: choose a fixed routine and trust it over the long haul.",
"lnmpair:whip|cross":"Effort that feels heavy and meaningful at once. The Whip and the Cross describe a duty you carry through repeated strain, like caring for a family obligation week after week. It asks what the work is for. Tip: share the load with someone.",
"lnmpair:birds|child":"Small chatter at the start of something. The Birds and the Child suggest a light conversation, a nervous but hopeful first step, or two beginners talking it over. Example: a first day anywhere new. Tip: keep expectations modest and let the ease grow naturally.",
"lnmpair:birds|fox":"Talk with an angle. The Birds and the Fox suggest office chatter, a conversation where someone is working an agenda, or nerves about a job matter. Example: a colleague who asks questions for their own benefit. Tip: listen for what is not said before answering.",
"lnmpair:birds|bear":"Reassuring conversation with a strong voice behind it. The Birds and the Bear point to a talk with a protector, a mentor or a generous relative, which settles the nerves. Tip: bring your actual worries to that person, not a polished version.",
"lnmpair:birds|stars":"Conversation that brings clarity. The Birds and the Stars suggest an encouraging talk, a hopeful exchange between two people, or nerves that ease once a plan takes shape. Example: a phone call after which the path looks plainer. Tip: write down what you hear.",
"lnmpair:birds|stork":"Chatter about a change. The Birds and the Stork describe a conversation about moving, switching routines or improving something, often with a flutter of nerves. Tip: talk it through with one other person before telling everyone, then let the plan settle.",
"lnmpair:birds|dog":"A trusted friend on the line. The Birds and the Dog point to a loyal conversation, two friends comparing notes, or reassurance from someone dependable. Example: a long evening call where nothing needs hiding. Tip: say the thing you have been rehearsing.",
"lnmpair:birds|tower":"Talk with formal weight, or talk in isolation. The Birds and the Tower can mean a conversation with an institution, a manager or an official office, or nervous thoughts spinning when alone. Tip: prepare questions before the meeting, and take a walk afterward.",
"lnmpair:birds|garden":"A buzz of social talk. The Birds and the Garden describe a party, a group chat or a gathering where everyone is speaking at once. Example: a neighborhood event. Tip: choose two conversations that matter and let the background noise go.",
"lnmpair:birds|mountain":"Nerves around an obstacle. The Birds and the Mountain suggest anxious talk about a delay, a hard conversation you keep postponing, or a blocked path being discussed again and again. Tip: name the one next step, however small, and say it out loud.",
"lnmpair:birds|crossroads":"Two voices and several roads. The Birds and the Crossroads describe a decision discussed with another person, with each option argued back and forth. Example: comparing two offers over dinner. Tip: set a date to decide, and list what each choice asks of you.",
"lnmpair:birds|mice":"Worry that nibbles. The Birds and the Mice point to anxious chatter, small repeated complaints, or a nagging thought you keep discussing. Example: overthinking a message you sent last week. Tip: write the worry down once, then give your attention to something concrete.",
"lnmpair:birds|heart":"Affectionate talk. The Birds and the Heart describe warm conversation, a loving pair, or the flutter of new fondness. Example: two people texting late at night. Tip: say something kind out loud today, since it carries further than you may expect.",
"lnmpair:birds|ring":"A conversation about commitment. The Birds and the Ring suggest talk of an agreement, a partnership or a deal two people are discussing, perhaps with nervous excitement. Tip: read what you are signing, and ask the question you are tempted to skip.",
"lnmpair:birds|book":"Whispers and things not yet explained. The Birds and the Book point to private talk, a subject people avoid, or two people studying something together. Example: a quiet exchange about a plan not yet announced. Tip: ask for the missing detail before guessing.",
"lnmpair:birds|letter":"Plenty of messages. The Birds and the Letter describe a stream of texts, calls and notes, often a little nervous in tone. Example: an inbox that fills while you wait for one reply. Tip: batch your replies into set times of day.",
"lnmpair:birds|man":"A person who talks, or who is talking over nerves. The Birds and the Man card describe the person asking, or someone significant, caught up in conversation. Example: rehearsing what to say to a manager. Tip: breathe, then say it plainly.",
"lnmpair:birds|woman":"Conversation centered on a significant person. The Birds and the Woman card suggest the person this card stands for is talking, listening or feeling a flutter of nerves. Example: a friend telling you about a tough week. Tip: let the pauses do some of the work.",
"lnmpair:birds|lily":"Gentle talk. The Birds and the Lily describe a peaceful conversation, a mature exchange between two people, or nerves settling into calm. Example: an older relative giving steady advice over tea. Tip: slow down your speech and the room follows.",
"lnmpair:birds|sun":"A cheerful exchange. The Birds and the Sun suggest good news shared aloud, confident speaking, or a conversation that leaves everyone warmer. Example: presenting an idea and getting an enthusiastic response. Tip: say yes to the invitation to speak up.",
"lnmpair:birds|moon":"Talk with feeling in it. The Birds and the Moon describe conversation touching emotions, or a message that brings recognition. Example: a colleague saying your work mattered. Tip: pay attention to the tone behind the words as well as the words.",
"lnmpair:birds|key":"A conversation that opens a door. The Birds and the Key point to an exchange that gives you the answer or the clear decision, like a call that settles a question. Tip: ask the direct question, since the other person may be ready to answer.",
"lnmpair:birds|fish":"Business talk with some jitters. The Birds and the Fish describe conversations about work, pricing or cash flow, often between two partners. Example: a call about a vendor and the terms. Tip: confirm numbers in writing so nothing stays vague.",
"lnmpair:birds|anchor":"Steady talk that lasts. The Birds and the Anchor suggest a dependable conversation, a long-term pair or nerves calmed by a firm routine. Example: a weekly call with a friend that always grounds you. Tip: keep the habit going.",
"lnmpair:birds|cross":"Heavy words, or words about a burden. The Birds and the Cross describe a serious conversation about duty or meaning, or worry about an obligation. Example: talking through a family responsibility. Tip: speak slowly and ask what support looks like.",
"lnmpair:child|fox":"A small beginning with some calculation in it. The Child and the Fox suggest a new job, a side project or a young venture where someone is being careful or a little sly. Example: a first freelance gig. Tip: stay observant, and trust your own read.",
"lnmpair:child|bear":"Something young under strong protection. The Child and the Bear describe a fresh start supported by a capable ally, a generous resource or a steady parent figure. Example: a new project backed by a patient mentor. Tip: accept help without apology.",
"lnmpair:child|stars":"A fresh start with a clear aim. The Child and the Stars suggest hope at the beginning of something, an idea that feels guided, or a beginner with real promise. Example: signing up for a course you have wanted for years. Tip: take the first small step.",
"lnmpair:child|stork":"A new start that brings change. The Child and the Stork describe a move, a fresh phase or a modest improvement that arrives gently. Example: starting a different routine in a new place. Tip: change one small habit first and see how it feels.",
"lnmpair:child|dog":"An innocent, loyal bond. The Child and the Dog point to a trusting friend, a new friendship or someone who stays by your side as something begins. Example: a classmate who saves you a seat. Tip: return that loyalty in plain, small ways.",
"lnmpair:child|tower":"A small start inside a large structure. The Child and the Tower describe a newcomer in an institution, a beginner meeting authority, or a quiet new chapter taken alone. Example: your first week at a big company. Tip: ask questions early.",
"lnmpair:child|garden":"A small gathering, or a first public step. The Child and the Garden suggest something new being shown to other people, like a first open mic or a launch party for a side project. Keep it modest and let a few friendly faces see it before a crowd does.",
"lnmpair:child|mountain":"A fresh beginning that meets a slow first stretch. The Child and the Mountain describe a new job, habit or plan that stalls early, like a draft that sits unfinished for weeks. Small, steady steps work better here than pushing hard for fast results.",
"lnmpair:child|crossroads":"Early options, and not yet much experience to judge them by. The Child and the Crossroads point to a first real choice, such as picking between two beginner courses. Try the smaller, reversible path first and see how it feels.",
"lnmpair:child|mice":"Small worries nibbling at something new. The Child and the Mice show a young project or idea losing its shine through minor doubts, like checking a new venture every hour for signs of trouble. Name the worry once, then give the idea some quiet room.",
"lnmpair:child|heart":"A tender, early feeling. The Child and the Heart point to affection that is just starting, or a sweet, simple warmth in a friendship or family bond. Be gentle with it. Nothing needs to be defined yet, and curiosity is plenty.",
"lnmpair:child|ring":"A new commitment, still finding its shape. The Child and the Ring suggest an early agreement, like a trial period, a first lease or a starter partnership. Read the terms slowly and remember that a young bond grows through small repeated gestures.",
"lnmpair:child|book":"Learning something from the very beginning. The Child and the Book describe a beginner course, a hidden talent just surfacing, or a private idea not ready to share. Protect that early stage and ask simple questions without apology.",
"lnmpair:child|letter":"A short, simple note, or the first draft of something. The Child and the Letter point to a brief message, an introductory email, or a young person writing to you. Keep the wording plain and honest, and skip the clever ornaments.",
"lnmpair:child|man":"Something new and small meets the person this card stands for. The Child and the Man card show a fresh start taken with a beginner's openness, like someone picking up a first instrument. Patience and play serve better than pressure here.",
"lnmpair:child|woman":"Here the person this card stands for steps into a young chapter, or nurtures a fledgling effort. The Child and the Woman card picture someone tending a new seedling project. Allow it to be imperfect while it finds its feet.",
"lnmpair:child|lily":"Calm, gentle beginnings. The Child and the Lily suggest a quiet start with a mature, peaceful tone, such as a slow new routine or a kind talk with a young relative. Resist the urge to hurry. Simple and unforced is the whole point.",
"lnmpair:child|sun":"A bright, easy start. The Child and the Sun describe a new venture, hobby or friendship that feels warm and encouraging from day one, like a first class that goes better than hoped. Enjoy it, and keep your expectations light.",
"lnmpair:child|moon":"A young talent or feeling gaining notice. The Child and the Moon point to a beginner being seen for their effort, or tender emotions surfacing around something new. Listen to your intuition about it, and share your progress with someone kind.",
"lnmpair:child|key":"A simple answer that opens a new door. The Child and the Key suggest a straightforward fix or a beginner insight, like finally understanding a basic step you skipped. Look for the small, obvious solution before building a complicated one.",
"lnmpair:child|fish":"A small venture with some flow to it. The Child and the Fish point to a modest business idea, a side hustle or a first sale, and how it is getting started. Keep records tidy and let it grow at its own natural speed.",
"lnmpair:child|anchor":"A young plan that wants roots. The Child and the Anchor describe a new thing being steadied through patience, such as a fresh routine that holds for months. Stay with the basics, repeat them daily, and let consistency do the quiet work.",
"lnmpair:child|cross":"A new start that carries some weight. The Child and the Cross suggest a fresh responsibility or a beginning that feels meaningful but heavy, like taking on a family duty. Ask for help early, and remember that small tasks add up.",
"lnmpair:fox|bear":"Clever work backed by real strength. The Fox and the Bear show a sharp strategy paired with solid resources, like a savvy colleague who also has the authority to act. Use skill and sturdiness together, and stay honest about what each is contributing.",
"lnmpair:fox|stars":"A clear idea in a murky workplace. The Fox and the Stars suggest guidance that helps you see through a tricky situation, such as spotting the real motive behind a vague request. Trust a hopeful plan, and double-check the small details.",
"lnmpair:fox|stork":"A shift in how work gets done. The Fox and the Stork point to a change of role, routine or approach that improves things if handled shrewdly, like moving to a team that suits you better. Plan the transition and keep your eyes open.",
"lnmpair:fox|dog":"Cunning meeting loyalty. The Fox and the Dog ask whether a trusted friend is giving plain counsel, or whether someone clever is playing the angles around that friend. Weigh the advice you hear against how reliable the speaker has been over time.",
"lnmpair:fox|tower":"Working inside a large structure. The Fox and the Tower describe office politics, a big institution, or a solitary job where you must be resourceful. Learn the unwritten rules, protect your own time, and stay quietly strategic.",
"lnmpair:fox|garden":"Work that happens in public. The Fox and the Garden suggest networking, events or a visible role where some careful self-presentation helps, like a trade fair booth. Be friendly, observe who is genuine, and keep your own aims in view.",
"lnmpair:fox|mountain":"A tricky obstacle at work. The Fox and the Mountain show a delay that calls for a clever workaround rather than brute force, like a stalled approval that needs a different route. Think sideways and stay patient with the process.",
"lnmpair:fox|crossroads":"A career choice that deserves scrutiny. The Fox and the Crossroads suggest several options where some hide fine print, such as two job offers with different catches. Read carefully, ask pointed questions, and pick the path that is honest in full.",
"lnmpair:fox|mice":"Slow leaks in working life. The Fox and the Mice point to small inefficiencies or petty irritations wearing down a project, like repeated tiny errors in a shared spreadsheet. Find the source, tighten up the routine, and do not let it drag your mood.",
"lnmpair:fox|heart":"Feelings mixed with strategy. The Fox and the Heart ask you to notice whether affection is plain or tangled up with an agenda, either yours or another person's. Speak openly, and let warmth be clear and uncalculated where it truly matters.",
"lnmpair:fox|ring":"A contract worth reading closely. The Fox and the Ring suggest an agreement, whether a job offer or a partnership, where details matter, such as who owns what. Take your time, ask questions, and sign only what you fully understand.",
"lnmpair:fox|book":"Hidden information at work. The Fox and the Book point to something being kept quiet, or to research that rewards a careful, shrewd look. Think of finding the buried clause in a long policy. Dig steadily and share what you learn wisely.",
"lnmpair:fox|letter":"A message with an angle. The Fox and the Letter suggest an email, document or application that needs a careful read, or one you should word with care, such as a cover letter. Check facts, keep copies, and read between the lines.",
"lnmpair:fox|man":"Resourcefulness, in the person this card stands for or in someone they deal with. The Fox and the Man card bring to mind a negotiator at the table. Stay alert, keep your own interests clear, and treat others with fairness.",
"lnmpair:fox|woman":"Shrewdness about work, in the person this card stands for or in a clever colleague nearby. The Fox and the Woman card picture someone quietly steering a project. Admire the skill, and keep your own communication direct.",
"lnmpair:fox|lily":"Wisdom tempering cleverness. The Fox and the Lily suggest working calmly and with integrity, so that skill serves peace rather than gamesmanship. A mature mentor may help here. Choose the honest option, even when a sly shortcut is on offer.",
"lnmpair:fox|sun":"Smart work in good light. The Fox and the Sun describe a clever approach being rewarded with visibility and energy, like a well-planned pitch landing warmly. Enjoy the momentum, stay transparent, and share credit with those who helped.",
"lnmpair:fox|moon":"Skill that earns recognition. The Fox and the Moon point to being noticed for work done cleverly, though emotions may run under the surface, such as nerves before a review. Trust your instincts, and be authentic as well as shrewd.",
"lnmpair:fox|key":"A clever solution at hand. The Fox and the Key show a smart fix or a way into a locked situation, like discovering the right contact for a stalled request. Act on it with confidence, but make sure it is honest and complete.",
"lnmpair:fox|fish":"Business sense in motion. The Fox and the Fish describe commerce, trade and a keen eye for how things flow, such as a freelancer who watches the market closely. Keep your practices clean, track the details, and stay open to fair partnerships.",
"lnmpair:fox|anchor":"Steady work that rewards savvy. The Fox and the Anchor suggest a long-term role or craft where cleverness is paired with persistence, like a seasoned worker who knows every shortcut. Build your reputation slowly and keep your methods transparent.",
"lnmpair:fox|cross":"A heavy workload that calls for strategy. The Fox and the Cross point to a duty or burden eased by smart planning, such as breaking a daunting task into stages. Look for what matters most, and decline the extras you cannot carry.",
"lnmpair:bear|stars":"Strength guided by a clear vision. The Bear and the Stars suggest a capable protector or resource lined up with real hope, like a mentor who helps set a goal and then supports it. Lean on solid backing and let the plan stay bright.",
"lnmpair:bear|stork":"Steady strength carries a shift. The Bear and the Stork describe a change that is well supported, like a move or a new role where you have backup, a mentor or a cushion behind you. Lean on what is solid before you let go of the old setup, and plan the practical details early.",
"lnmpair:bear|dog":"A strong, dependable friend. The Bear and the Dog suggest a loyal ally with real weight behind them, the sort who helps you move house and never mentions it again. Ask for their view on a hard question, and return the loyalty in kind.",
"lnmpair:bear|tower":"Big institutions and a great deal of authority. The Bear and the Tower can point to a large employer, a university, a bank or a government office. Expect rules and slow paperwork. Go in prepared, keep copies of everything, and respect the chain of command.",
"lnmpair:bear|garden":"Strength shown in public. The Bear and the Garden suggest a person or group with real presence at gatherings, events or online communities. Think of the organiser who quietly holds a whole party together. Show up, be visible, and let your reliability speak for you.",
"lnmpair:bear|mountain":"A heavy obstacle met with heavy resources. The Bear and the Mountain say the delay is real but so is your staying power. Picture a long project stuck in review. Pace yourself, gather your backing, and look for progress in small, solid steps instead of one big push.",
"lnmpair:bear|crossroads":"A choice that deserves a sturdy footing. The Bear and the Crossroads ask what you can rely on before you pick a path. Perhaps two job offers, one with better support. Count your real resources, then choose the route you can sustain.",
"lnmpair:bear|mice":"Strength being worn down in small ways. The Bear and the Mice suggest a resource nibbled at by constant little drains, like forgotten subscriptions, piling tasks, or a helper stretched thin. Find the leaks, patch them one at a time, and rest your reserves.",
"lnmpair:bear|heart":"Protective, generous love. The Bear and the Heart describe affection that shows itself through care and practical help, like the partner or parent who fixes the car and packs the lunch. Say the warm words out loud too, so the care is never mistaken for duty.",
"lnmpair:bear|ring":"A serious, binding agreement. The Bear and the Ring point to a commitment with weight, such as a long contract, a partnership or a promise made to last. Read every clause before signing. Choose partners who are as steady as you intend to be.",
"lnmpair:bear|book":"Power held in reserve and knowledge kept quiet. The Bear and the Book suggest someone strong who does not explain everything, or a subject you must study before acting. Do the reading first. Keep your own plans private until you understand the terrain.",
"lnmpair:bear|letter":"A weighty message or document. The Bear and the Letter often mean an official notice, a formal agreement or a note from someone in a position of strength. Read it in full and keep a copy. Reply calmly, in writing, once you have thought it through.",
"lnmpair:bear|man":"The person asking, or someone important to them, is carrying a lot. The Bear and the Man card speak of capability, protection and a habit of shouldering things alone. Accept help when it is offered. Strength is easier to keep when it is shared.",
"lnmpair:bear|woman":"Steadiness and good resources belong to the person asking, or someone near them. The Bear and the Woman card show a capable presence who looks after others and holds the household or team together. Notice what that role costs, and make space for rest.",
"lnmpair:bear|lily":"Calm, mature power. The Bear and the Lily describe a gentle strength, the elder who settles an argument just by walking into the room. Seek advice from someone with that kind of settled experience, and try to answer provocation with patience.",
"lnmpair:bear|sun":"Confidence with plenty of energy to back it. The Bear and the Sun describe a bright, productive stretch, with a capable person or plan that simply works. Use the momentum on something that matters to you, and thank the people who made it possible.",
"lnmpair:bear|moon":"Respect earned through steady work. The Bear and the Moon suggest recognition for reliability, or a strong person with a tender inner life. Let your sensitivity be seen alongside your competence. Trust a gut feeling when it matches what the facts say.",
"lnmpair:bear|key":"A solid answer within reach. The Bear and the Key suggest that the right resource or the right helper opens the door, such as a trusted adviser who finally explains the process. Ask the capable person directly instead of struggling alone.",
"lnmpair:bear|fish":"Substantial resources in motion. The Bear and the Fish speak of business that has weight behind it, like an established company with reliable cash flow. Keep records tidy, steer by your plan, and stay measured about how much you commit.",
"lnmpair:bear|anchor":"Deep, lasting stability. The Bear and the Anchor describe a foundation that holds in rough weather, like a long marriage, a career of many years or a home that has stayed put. Value what endures, and keep tending it so it stays strong.",
"lnmpair:bear|cross":"A large responsibility that has meaning. The Bear and the Cross suggest you are carrying a duty that is heavy, but you are built for it, such as caring for a family member or leading a hard project. Set limits, and share the load where you can.",
"lnmpair:stars|stork":"Hope with a change attached. The Stars and the Stork point to a clear, improving shift, like a better job, a move toward a place that feels right, or a fresh stage after a long wait. Keep your goal in view, and take the next small practical step toward it.",
"lnmpair:stars|dog":"A friend who offers clear guidance. The Stars and the Dog describe a trustworthy person whose advice actually helps, like the old colleague who tells you straight what they see. Listen to their view, and be honest with them in return.",
"lnmpair:stars|tower":"Clarity found in formal places or in solitude. The Stars and the Tower suggest guidance coming from an institution, a mentor with standing or some quiet time alone. A course, an appointment or a long walk by yourself can sharpen the picture.",
"lnmpair:stars|garden":"Hopes made visible in company. The Stars and the Garden suggest your best ideas gain shape around other people, at a meetup, a class, an event or an online group. Share your plan openly and notice who responds. Good contacts often arrive this way.",
"lnmpair:stars|mountain":"A guiding light on a hard stretch. The Stars and the Mountain say the obstacle is real, yet there is a visible way through. Think of a long exam course with a clear syllabus. Break the climb into stages, and keep the far goal in sight.",
"lnmpair:stars|crossroads":"Clarity about a choice. The Stars and the Crossroads suggest that when you are honest about your hopes, the right path shows itself. Write down what you truly want from each option. The answer is often the one that feels clearest, not the loudest.",
"lnmpair:stars|mice":"Hope eroded by worry, or worry eased by perspective. The Stars and the Mice show a clear aim being nibbled at by small doubts. Name the specific concern, deal with the one that is practical, and return your attention to the long view.",
"lnmpair:stars|heart":"Hopeful, open affection. The Stars and the Heart describe warmth guided by honest intention, like a new connection where both sides say what they want. Keep faith in what you feel, and let your actions show that your care is sincere.",
"lnmpair:stars|ring":"A hopeful commitment. The Stars and the Ring suggest an agreement or partnership built on a shared vision, such as a joint venture or a promise that has a clear purpose. Be sure the goals match on paper as well as in feeling.",
"lnmpair:stars|book":"Illumination through study. The Stars and the Book describe a subject finally making sense, like a hard course that clicks after weeks of reading, or a secret that comes clear. Keep studying, and take notes, because insight deepens with review.",
"lnmpair:stars|letter":"Clear, encouraging words. The Stars and the Letter suggest a message that brings direction, such as a helpful reply, an acceptance, or a plan set down in plain terms. Write your own goals in a short note, and send that email you have been delaying.",
"lnmpair:stars|man":"The person asking, or someone significant to them, is looking for direction. The Stars and the Man card show a seeker with ideals and a sense of purpose. Pick one guiding aim for this season, and measure your choices against it.",
"lnmpair:stars|woman":"Plenty of vision sits with the person asking, or someone close to them. The Stars and the Woman card describe a clear thinker with a purpose, who may be the guide others turn to. Trust that inner compass, and give your ideas room to grow.",
"lnmpair:stars|lily":"Quiet clarity and peace of mind. The Stars and the Lily point to a settled, wise outlook, like the calm after a long debate is resolved. Choose steady, honest routes over drama, and give yourself a little stillness to think.",
"lnmpair:stars|sun":"A bright outlook with real direction. The Stars and the Sun suggest confidence that rests on a clear plan, like a project whose purpose finally feels obvious. Put your energy into the goal you believe in, and share the good mood generously.",
"lnmpair:stars|moon":"Inspiration and recognition. The Stars and the Moon point to creative, intuitive insight, perhaps a talent noticed by others or a dream worth acting on. Keep a notebook beside your bed, and trust the ideas that return again and again.",
"lnmpair:stars|key":"The answer is available. The Stars and the Key describe clarity that unlocks a problem, like the moment a confusing situation finally makes sense. Trust the insight, act on it in a practical way, and share it with whoever needs it.",
"lnmpair:stars|fish":"Guided effort in work and resources. The Stars and the Fish suggest a business or money question that benefits from a clear plan and a long view. Set sensible targets, review them often, and keep your values in sight when you weigh options.",
"lnmpair:stars|anchor":"A goal worth sticking with. The Stars and the Anchor describe hope paired with perseverance, like years of steady practice toward a craft or qualification. Keep your routine going on dull days, because consistency turns a vision into something real.",
"lnmpair:stars|cross":"Meaning found in a burden. The Stars and the Cross suggest a duty that feels lighter once you see its purpose, such as caring work or a demanding commitment. Ask what you are serving, and let that answer steer you on the hard days.",
"lnmpair:stork|dog":"A trusted friend arrives with a change, or a change is made easier by someone loyal. The Stork and the Dog read like a reliable person helping you through a move or a new chapter. Example: a friend offers a spare room while you relocate. Say yes to the help.",
"lnmpair:stork|tower":"Change meets a big institution, or a quiet stretch alone. The Stork and the Tower can describe a transfer inside a large organization, a new rule to adapt to, or a move toward more solitude. Read the paperwork carefully and give yourself time to settle.",
"lnmpair:stork|garden":"Improvement shows up in your social world. The Stork and the Garden suggest a fresh crowd, a new group, or a gathering that marks a turn for the better. Example: a neighborhood event where you meet someone useful. Go out and be seen.",
"lnmpair:stork|mountain":"A change that moves slowly. The Stork and the Mountain describe an improvement held up by a delay, a stubborn obstacle, or a long climb before the new situation settles. Example: a move pushed back by a late approval. Pace yourself and keep going.",
"lnmpair:stork|crossroads":"Change comes with options attached. The Stork and the Crossroads point to a move or upgrade where you still have more than one path to pick from. Example: two offers in two different cities. List what matters most before you choose.",
"lnmpair:stork|mice":"A change that gets nibbled at by worry. The Stork and the Mice suggest an improvement shadowed by small stresses, slow leaks of energy, or doubts that wear on your nerves. Example: a happy move and a growing pile of little chores. Handle one small thing daily.",
"lnmpair:stork|heart":"Warmth is on the move. The Stork and the Heart point to affection changing shape: a new bond, a deeper stage, or feelings that arrive with a fresh start. Example: a long-distance friend moving closer. Let the new warmth be as gentle as it needs to be.",
"lnmpair:stork|ring":"Change tied to a commitment. Read the Stork and the Ring as a new agreement, a contract renewed on better terms, or a cycle that turns over. Example: signing a lease in a new place. Read the fine print and know what you are agreeing to.",
"lnmpair:stork|book":"A fresh start built on something not yet known. The Stork and the Book suggest a change that comes with learning, a hidden detail, or a course of study. Example: a new job where the first month is mostly training. Stay curious and ask questions early.",
"lnmpair:stork|letter":"News of a change, often in writing. The Stork and the Letter can mean an email announcing a move, a form that confirms an improvement, or a message with a new direction in it. Open it soon, and keep a copy of anything official.",
"lnmpair:stork|man":"The person asking, or someone close to you, is in motion. The Stork and the Man card describe that person changing place, role or outlook, usually for the better. Example: a sibling taking a post in another town. Support the transition and stay in touch.",
"lnmpair:stork|woman":"The person asking, or someone close to you, is in the middle of a shift. The Stork and the Woman card point to a new role, a new home, or an upgraded point of view. Example: a coworker stepping into a bigger job. Give the change room to land.",
"lnmpair:stork|lily":"A calm kind of change. The Stork and the Lily suggest improvement arriving gently, with maturity and little drama. Example: a household easing into a quieter routine after a move. Resist stirring things up just to feel progress, since peace is the point here.",
"lnmpair:stork|sun":"Change that lifts your energy. The Stork and the Sun point to a fresh start with good light on it: confidence, visible progress, a season that feels easier. Example: a new schedule that finally suits you. Enjoy the lift and act while the momentum is warm.",
"lnmpair:stork|moon":"A shift that others notice. The Stork and the Moon suggest recognition tied to a change, along with a strong emotional tide around it. Example: praise after you take on a new role. Trust your gut about what feels right, and give your moods a day to settle.",
"lnmpair:stork|key":"A change that unlocks something. The Stork and the Key describe a move or improvement that opens a door, answers a question, or makes a stuck situation workable. Example: a new address that finally solves the commute. Use the opening while it is clear.",
"lnmpair:stork|fish":"Change flowing through your resources. The Stork and the Fish suggest a shift in how your business or income moves, such as new channels, new clients or a new rhythm. Keep records tidy while things move, and treat this as a pattern to watch, not a promise.",
"lnmpair:stork|anchor":"Change that is meant to last. The Stork and the Anchor describe a move or improvement aimed at steadier ground, like a settled job or a home you plan to stay in. Example: finally finishing a relocation. Put down routines that hold you.",
"lnmpair:stork|cross":"Change carried with a sense of duty. The Stork and the Cross suggest a transition that feels heavy or meaningful, perhaps one you take on for others. Example: moving to look after family matters. Share the load where you can, and name what the effort is for.",
"lnmpair:dog|tower":"A loyal friend inside a larger structure. The Dog and the Tower can describe a trusted ally at work, a mentor in an institution, or a friend who keeps their own distance. Example: a colleague who vouches for you with management. Respect their boundaries.",
"lnmpair:dog|garden":"Friendship in public. The Dog and the Garden point to a loyal companion in your social circle, a gathering of trusted people, or a community that has your back. Example: a friend who introduces you around at a party. Show up for them in return.",
"lnmpair:dog|mountain":"A friend meets a big obstacle, or loyalty is tested by delay. The Dog and the Mountain suggest a faithful person stuck behind a hurdle, or trust that needs patience. Example: a friend who cannot help right now. Be understanding, and ask what they can offer.",
"lnmpair:dog|crossroads":"A trusted person at a fork. The Dog and the Crossroads describe a friend who helps you weigh options, or a loyalty that has to choose a side. Example: asking a good friend which job to take. Listen to their view, then decide for yourself.",
"lnmpair:dog|mice":"Trust wearing thin in small ways. The Dog and the Mice suggest worry about a friend, or a loyal bond strained by little neglects. Example: a promise slipping again and again. Speak up early and kindly before the small things pile into something heavier.",
"lnmpair:dog|heart":"Loving loyalty. The Dog and the Heart read as a dear friend, a devoted bond, or affection built on years of trust. Example: someone who simply shows up when things are hard. Tell the people you trust that you value them, out loud.",
"lnmpair:dog|ring":"A trusted bond made formal. The Dog and the Ring suggest a promise between friends, a partnership held in good faith, or a loyal agreement that comes back around. Example: a business pact with an old friend. Write down what you each expect.",
"lnmpair:dog|book":"A friend who keeps something back. The Dog and the Book point to a confidant, a study partner, or loyalty tied to a secret. Example: a friend who knows more than they say. Trust them with questions, and respect what they hold private.",
"lnmpair:dog|letter":"A message from, or about, a trusted person. The Dog and the Letter suggest a note from a friend, a reference you can rely on, or a written pledge. Example: a recommendation sent on your behalf. Answer a message from a friend soon, since it matters to them.",
"lnmpair:dog|man":"A faithful ally stands near the person asking. The Dog and the Man card describe a friend or supporter close to you, someone steady who backs your plans. Example: a pal who reviews your plan before you share it. Lean on them, and return the favor.",
"lnmpair:dog|woman":"A dependable friend stands beside the person asking. The Dog and the Woman card describe a loyal supporter or confidant in your life, whatever their gender. Example: a neighbor who checks on you. Say thank you plainly and keep the friendship two-sided.",
"lnmpair:dog|lily":"Friendship that has matured. The Dog and the Lily suggest a long-standing bond, quiet trust, and a peaceful understanding that needs no proof. Example: an old friend you can sit in silence with. Protect the calm between you and avoid stirring up old quarrels.",
"lnmpair:dog|sun":"A bright, generous friend. The Dog and the Sun point to loyal support that lifts your mood, or a friendship in a happy season. Example: a buddy who cheers you on at an event. Share your good news with them, and let them share theirs.",
"lnmpair:dog|moon":"A friend who sees you clearly. The Dog and the Moon suggest an intuitive, caring person who notices your feelings and may give you credit when it counts. Example: a friend who senses your mood from one text. Trust their read, and be honest in return.",
"lnmpair:dog|key":"A trusted person holds the solution. The Dog and the Key describe a friend who opens a door, a dependable answer, or loyalty that settles a question. Example: a contact who finally gets you in the room. Accept the help gratefully and keep your word.",
"lnmpair:dog|fish":"Loyalty meets business. The Dog and the Fish suggest a trustworthy partner, a reliable client, or a friend connected to your work. Example: a long-time customer who refers you to others. Keep dealings honest, and keep friendship and paperwork clearly separate.",
"lnmpair:dog|anchor":"A friend for the long haul. The Dog and the Anchor point to steady loyalty, a bond that holds through changes, or trust that grows slowly over time. Example: a friend present across many years. Keep nurturing it with small, regular check-ins.",
"lnmpair:dog|cross":"Loyalty carries a weight. The Dog and the Cross suggest a friend who shares a burden, or a duty to stand by someone through a hard stretch. Example: helping a friend with a heavy responsibility. Give what you can, and keep your own limits in sight.",
"lnmpair:tower|garden":"A formal place opens to the public. The Tower and the Garden suggest an institution reaching out, an organized event, or a person alone stepping into company. Example: a school hosting an open house. Go in with a clear aim and leave when you have what you came for.",
"lnmpair:tower|mountain":"A rigid system meets a big obstacle. The Tower and the Mountain describe bureaucracy, a slow approval, or isolation that feels hard to shift. Example: a request stuck in a department for weeks. Keep paperwork organized, follow up politely, and be patient.",
"lnmpair:tower|crossroads":"A choice involving authority or solitude. The Tower and the Crossroads suggest options inside an institution, or a decision best made alone. Example: choosing between two programs at a university. Gather the official facts, then take quiet time to decide.",
"lnmpair:tower|mice":"Slow erosion inside a large institution: the Tower and the Mice point to worry that gnaws at a formal setting, such as a workplace where small rules and small slights pile up. Name the minor irritations early and write them down. Tiny leaks are easier to patch than a cracked wall.",
"lnmpair:tower|heart":"Warmth inside a formal or isolated place. The Tower and the Heart can describe affection that grows at a school, office or hospital, or a loving person who keeps a little distance. Example: a kind coworker you only see at the weekly meeting. Let the feeling show in small, steady ways.",
"lnmpair:tower|ring":"A formal agreement with an institution, or a promise kept in private. The Tower and the Ring suggest a contract, membership or vow that carries official weight. Read every clause before you sign anything. Ask who holds the authority behind the paper and what the agreement asks of you.",
"lnmpair:tower|book":"Closed doors and sealed knowledge. The Tower and the Book hint at something studied in an institution, or a matter kept confidential, such as a thesis in review or a file only a few can open. Be patient with what stays hidden, and ask plainly for the access you need.",
"lnmpair:tower|letter":"An official notice. The Tower and the Letter read as a formal document: a form from an office, a reply from a school, a bank statement or a stern email from a manager. Read it twice, keep a copy, and answer in the same calm, correct tone it used.",
"lnmpair:tower|man":"Authority or solitude around the person asked about. The Tower and the Man card describe someone who works within a system, keeps their own counsel, or stands apart from the crowd. Example: a supervisor, or yourself on a quiet retreat. Respect the need for space, and keep boundaries clear.",
"lnmpair:tower|woman":"A person of standing, or a person who prefers distance. The Tower and the Woman card can show someone in an official role, or someone who walks alone by choice. Example: a principal, a director, or you needing a quiet week. Approach with courtesy, and do not push past limits.",
"lnmpair:tower|lily":"Calm authority and a settled, quiet place. The Tower and the Lily suggest maturity within a formal structure, like a respected elder at an old institution or a peaceful retreat. Example: a library afternoon. Lean into unhurried routines and let the stillness do some of the work.",
"lnmpair:tower|sun":"Visible success within a structure. The Tower and the Sun point to energy that rises through formal channels: recognition at a large organization, a good review, a clear and well-lit path through an application. Use the momentum, but keep following the official steps rather than skipping them.",
"lnmpair:tower|moon":"Being noticed by the institution, or feelings that rise in solitude. The Tower and the Moon can mean recognition from an organization, or tender moods in a quiet setting. Example: praise from a board, or an evening alone that surfaces what you really feel. Write both down.",
"lnmpair:tower|key":"A way through a closed system. The Tower and the Key suggest the right access: an authorized contact, the correct form, or a rule that quietly allows exceptions. Example: asking the registrar the one question that unlocks the process. Find the person who knows how the building actually works.",
"lnmpair:tower|fish":"Business within a large structure. The Tower and the Fish describe how resources move through an organization: budgets, payroll, institutional accounts or a corporate flow. Example: learning how your department's funds are approved. Study the process before asking, and keep clear records of what passes through.",
"lnmpair:tower|anchor":"Lasting standing in a solid institution. The Tower and the Anchor suggest long service, tenure or a place you can hold for years, like a steady post at an established firm. Tip: stability is a real asset, so check whether you are staying from loyalty or only from habit.",
"lnmpair:tower|cross":"A heavy duty carried in a formal setting. The Tower and the Cross read as responsibility that feels imposed from above, such as a mandatory role at work, or a long stretch of loneliness with a purpose behind it. Ask what meaning it holds for you, and share the load where you can.",
"lnmpair:garden|mountain":"A social event that meets a snag. The Garden and the Mountain describe a gathering delayed, a crowd that is hard to reach or a public project stuck behind a big obstacle. Example: a launch party held up by permits. Scale down, pace yourself, and keep the guest list in mind.",
"lnmpair:garden|crossroads":"Choices about where to be seen. The Garden and the Crossroads show a decision between social paths: which group to join, which invitation to accept, which stage to step onto. Example: two weekend events on the same night. List what each offers you before you answer either host.",
"lnmpair:garden|mice":"Social worry wearing things down. The Garden and the Mice suggest small stresses in public life: nagging gossip, awkward group chats, or a crowded event that leaves you drained. Example: dreading the office party. Leave early without apology, and protect your energy before it frays further.",
"lnmpair:garden|heart":"Affection in company. The Garden and the Heart point to love found among friends, at parties, or in a community space. Example: a warm conversation at a neighbor's cookout. Go to the gathering, stay open, and let friendly feeling develop at its own pace without forcing it.",
"lnmpair:garden|ring":"A public commitment. The Garden and the Ring suggest a formal group promise, a membership, or a ceremony witnessed by others, such as a wedding-style gathering or signing up with a club. Pay attention to what you promise in front of people, since it is easy to say yes in a crowd.",
"lnmpair:garden|book":"Learning among others, and things not yet said in public. The Garden and the Book can show a class, a reading group or a workshop, or a secret circulating quietly through a social circle. Example: a study meetup. Share what you know generously, and keep private what is not yours to tell.",
"lnmpair:garden|letter":"News reaching a wider audience. The Garden and the Letter point to an invitation, a public announcement, a newsletter or a message sent to many people at once. Example: a flyer for a community event. Check the date and place twice, and reply promptly if you were asked to confirm.",
"lnmpair:garden|man":"The person in question is out among people. The Garden and the Man card show someone social, or the person asking at a party, a market or a club. Example: a friend who knows everyone at the gathering. Say hello, ask for an introduction, and listen before you pitch yourself.",
"lnmpair:garden|woman":"Someone well connected. The Garden and the Woman card suggest a person at home in company, or you stepping into a visible role, such as a host, an organizer or a community voice. Example: the one who remembers every name. Accept the spotlight lightly, and keep room for quiet time afterward.",
"lnmpair:garden|lily":"A relaxed, harmonious gathering. The Garden and the Lily suggest gentle company, such as a family picnic, an easygoing dinner with older friends, or a mellow community event. Let the pace stay slow. Choose settings where nobody needs to perform, and enjoy the plain pleasure of sitting together.",
"lnmpair:garden|sun":"A bright, well-attended occasion. The Garden and the Sun suggest popularity, visibility and good cheer in public: a festival, a successful open house, or an event that fills up with friendly faces. Show up, share the credit, and let your enthusiasm be seen by the people around you.",
"lnmpair:garden|moon":"Being noticed by the crowd. The Garden and the Moon describe recognition in public life, along with the moods that come with it: a post that gets attention, a role that brings applause, or a tender feeling in a roomful of people. Check in with yourself before you step out.",
"lnmpair:garden|key":"An opening through other people. The Garden and the Key suggest that a social connection unlocks the answer: a friend of a friend, a chat at a mixer, or a community contact. Example: a tip passed along at a barbecue. Say what you are looking for out loud.",
"lnmpair:garden|fish":"Business that grows through networking. The Garden and the Fish show resources flowing through public life: customers who come from word of mouth, a market stall, or a trade event. Example: handing out a card at a fair. Be generous with introductions and keep a tidy record of your contacts.",
"lnmpair:garden|anchor":"A lasting place in a community. The Garden and the Anchor point to long-term belonging: a club you have kept up for years, a neighborhood you are settling into, or a reputation built steadily in public. Keep showing up regularly, since trust among people builds through repetition.",
"lnmpair:garden|cross":"A public duty. The Garden and the Cross suggest a burden carried in front of others, like organizing a charity event, a volunteer role, or a family gathering that depends on you. Example: hosting every holiday. Ask for help, and decide which obligations still carry meaning for you.",
"lnmpair:mountain|crossroads":"A difficult choice, or a blocked path with alternatives. The Mountain and the Crossroads suggest that one route is steep and another may be gentler, such as a slow application versus a different program. Weigh the effort each demands, and be honest about which hurdle is actually worth climbing.",
"lnmpair:mountain|mice":"A large obstacle ground down by small worries, or small worries growing into a looming one. The Mountain and the Mice suggest stress that wears at you during a long delay. Example: waiting on a stalled project. Break the task into small steps and handle one before you check the others.",
"lnmpair:mountain|heart":"Affection meeting resistance. The Mountain and the Heart describe a feeling held back by distance, timing or stubbornness, or a hard problem approached with warmth. Example: caring for someone who is slow to open up. Stay patient and kind, and keep your own needs in view as well.",
"lnmpair:mountain|ring":"A commitment that comes with a challenge, or an agreement that stalls. The Mountain and the Ring suggest a contract with obstacles, or a long cycle that keeps returning to the same hill. Example: renegotiating a lease. Read the terms again, and be realistic about the time it takes.",
"lnmpair:mountain|book":"A hard subject, or a secret behind a wall. The Mountain and the Book point to demanding study, such as a tough course or an exam, or information that is difficult to reach. Pace your study in short sessions, and ask a teacher or mentor to show you the way up.",
"lnmpair:mountain|letter":"A delayed or difficult message. The Mountain and the Letter suggest a reply that is slow to arrive, paperwork stuck in a queue, or a document that carries unwelcome news. Example: an overdue answer from an office. Follow up politely, keep a record, and avoid sending a message in frustration.",
"lnmpair:mountain|man":"A stubborn or blocked person, or the asker facing a hurdle. The Mountain and the Man card suggest someone who is hard to move, or you confronting something large. Example: a rigid supervisor, or your own resistance. Approach calmly, keep your requests specific, and do not push on a closed door.",
"lnmpair:mountain|woman":"Someone firm, or a person meeting a big challenge. The Mountain and the Woman card show a person who holds their ground, or you working through an obstacle that asks for endurance. Example: a relative with strong opinions. Give it time, stay steady and choose which hills deserve a stand.",
"lnmpair:mountain|lily":"Patience brings calm after a long climb. The Mountain and the Lily suggest a challenge handled with maturity and peace, such as a long delay faced with composure. Example: a slow process you have learned to accept. Keep your pace gentle and trust the quiet strength you have built.",
"lnmpair:mountain|sun":"Energy that clears an obstacle. The Mountain and the Sun suggest effort that eventually pays off: a hard project that begins to show results, or morale returning after a long delay. Example: a big task finally in sight of the top. Keep your momentum, and enjoy the view on the way.",
"lnmpair:mountain|moon":"A wall in front of a spotlight. The Mountain and the Moon suggest a delay in being noticed, or feelings that feel blocked from showing. Think of a good idea that sits unseen in a shared folder for weeks. Tip: keep doing the quiet work and let the credit arrive on its own schedule.",
"lnmpair:mountain|key":"Here is a hard problem with a real way through. The Mountain and the Key point to a stubborn obstacle that finally has an answer, though not an instant one. Picture a tangled paperwork issue where one phone call to the right department clears it. Tip: look for the person or step that actually holds the answer.",
"lnmpair:mountain|fish":"Slow water. The Mountain and the Fish describe money or business flow that is held up, such as a client who keeps postponing the invoice sign-off. It reads as a delay, not a verdict. Tip: widen the channels you rely on so one blocked stream does not stall everything.",
"lnmpair:mountain|anchor":"Hard, patient ground. The Mountain and the Anchor show a long climb that rewards those who stay put and keep steady. Think of a degree, a renovation, or a slow-building skill that tests your stamina. Tip: measure progress by small steady steps instead of by how far the summit still looks.",
"lnmpair:mountain|cross":"A heavy load on a steep path. The Mountain and the Cross read as a difficult duty or a stretch that feels bigger than it is fair. An example is caring for a family obligation while work piles up. Tip: ask what can be shared or set down, and who could carry part of it.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|mice":"Choices get nibbled away by overthinking. The Crossroads and the Mice describe a decision that wears you down because you keep circling it. Think of comparing two apartments at midnight for the fifth night running. Tip: set a deadline, name your top two criteria, and stop gathering more input.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|heart":"Two paths, and your feelings pull on the choice. The Crossroads and the Heart point to a decision shaped by affection, such as whether to move closer to someone you care about. Tip: let what warms you count as real information, then check it against practical details too.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|ring":"A fork that leads toward commitment. The Crossroads and the Ring suggest choosing between binding agreements, such as two job contracts or whether to formalise a partnership. Tip: read the terms closely and picture an ordinary Tuesday inside each option before you sign anything.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|book":"Options exist that you cannot fully see yet. The Crossroads and the Book mean a choice made harder by missing information. Maybe a course, a company, or a plan hides details until you ask. Tip: do some real research, ask direct questions, and decide once the gaps close.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|letter":"A decision arrives in writing, or needs to be. The Crossroads and the Letter point to a message, offer, or form that presents you with options. Think of two replies waiting in your inbox. Tip: draft your answer, sleep on it, and send it only when it sounds like you.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|man":"The person asking, or someone close, stands at a fork. The Crossroads and the Man card show a person weighing options and looking for direction. It could be you deciding about a move or a friend asking your opinion. Tip: list the real choices on paper and talk them through aloud.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|woman":"More than one road lies ahead for you or someone close. The Crossroads and the Woman card show a person who has options and needs to pick a direction. Perhaps it is a role change or a relocation. Tip: separate what you want from what others expect, then choose.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|lily":"A calm fork. The Crossroads and the Lily suggest a choice you can make without pressure, usually from a mature, settled place. Several paths may all be decent. Tip: go for the option that keeps your days peaceful and your relationships easy, and trust that there is no wrong door here.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|sun":"Bright options. The Crossroads and the Sun point to a choice where more than one route looks promising, and energy runs high. Think of two offers that both excite you. Tip: confidence is a good guide here, but test each path against one practical question before committing.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|moon":"Intuition votes at the fork. The Crossroads and the Moon suggest that feelings and recognition guide a decision, perhaps a path that earns you visibility. Think of choosing the project people will notice. Tip: tune in to which option leaves you calmer, and back that instinct with a quick fact check.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|key":"A choice that unlocks something. The Crossroads and the Key point to a decision that opens a door, with one option clearly more useful than the rest. Picture finally picking the program that gets you where you wanted to go. Tip: ask which path solves the actual problem, then take it.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|fish":"Money and business have more than one route. The Crossroads and the Fish describe a decision about income, trade, or how to spend resources. An example is picking between steady pay and a flexible venture. Tip: compare each option over a full year, not just the first month.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|anchor":"A fork that favors staying power. The Crossroads and the Anchor suggest choosing the road that gives lasting stability over the flashier one. Think of weighing a safe long-term plan against a quick change. Tip: pick what you can still stand behind in five years.",
"lnmpair:crossroads|cross":"A choice that carries weight. The Crossroads and the Cross point to a decision tied to duty or meaning, where no option feels light. It could be whether to take on a family responsibility. Tip: choose according to your values, accept the cost openly, and ask for help with the load.",
"lnmpair:mice|heart":"Small worries gnaw at affection. The Mice and the Heart describe love worn down by little things, like unspoken irritation or constant doubt about whether you are cared for. Tip: name the small thing out loud early, kindly, before it grows into a wall between you.",
"lnmpair:mice|ring":"A commitment slowly eroding. The Mice and the Ring point to a contract or promise that is fraying through neglect, such as a subscription, a lease, or a partnership nobody tends. Tip: review the agreement now, fix the small leaks, and have the honest conversation before the gap widens.",
"lnmpair:mice|book":"Worry feeds on what is unknown. The Mice and the Book suggest anxiety growing around a secret, an exam, or something you have not yet studied. Think of dreading a topic you have not opened. Tip: read the thing, ask the question, and let facts shrink the fear.",
"lnmpair:mice|letter":"A message that nags. The Mice and the Letter describe stress around a document, email, or bill that you keep avoiding, or a note that frays your nerves. Tip: open it today, answer in two sentences, and file it, because dread usually outweighs the task itself.",
"lnmpair:mice|man":"Someone is being worn down, either you or a person near you. The Mice and the Man card show a person under steady stress or slow loss, such as a long stretch of overwork. Tip: find the one small leak that drains the most, patch it first, and rest on purpose.",
"lnmpair:mice|woman":"Frayed nerves, yours or those of someone close. The Mice and the Woman card show a person carrying accumulated stress, maybe from many small demands. Tip: trim the list down to what matters, say no to one thing this week, and let someone else pick up a task.",
"lnmpair:mice|lily":"Peace under pressure. The Mice and the Lily suggest that calm and maturity can soothe small worries, or that nagging stress is disturbing a settled time. Picture a quiet household unsettled by a minor repair. Tip: handle the minor annoyance promptly so the calm can return.",
"lnmpair:mice|sun":"Light on what has been eating at you. The Mice and the Sun mean worries ease when exposed to energy and openness, and a drained stretch begins to recover. Think of finally airing a long-held concern. Tip: say it aloud, get outside, and let warmth do some of the work.",
"lnmpair:mice|moon":"Anxious feelings and little losses of confidence. The Mice and the Moon describe worry seeping into your mood and your sense of recognition, like feeling overlooked after repeated small slights. Tip: write down what is really bothering you, then separate the facts from the story you add.",
"lnmpair:mice|key":"A fix for a slow leak. The Mice and the Key point to finding the answer to a gradual problem, such as the exact habit costing you time or goods. Tip: track the pattern for a week, spot the one cause, and act on that, rather than patching everything at once.",
"lnmpair:mice|fish":"Money trickles away in small amounts. The Mice and the Fish suggest minor leaks in business or spending, like forgotten fees or slow customers. Tip: go through recurring charges and small habits one line at a time, since steady attention usually stops the loss.",
"lnmpair:mice|anchor":"Wear on something meant to last. The Mice and the Anchor describe stability being tested by small stresses, like a routine slowly slipping or a dependable setup showing cracks. Tip: do maintenance early, in short regular sessions, so the foundation stays firm.",
"lnmpair:mice|cross":"Worry stacked on a burden. The Mice and the Cross point to a heavy duty made heavier by constant fretting, such as a long caregiving stretch with endless small tasks. Tip: split the load into pieces, accept help, and watch for rest breaks that restore you.",
"lnmpair:heart|ring":"Affection that seeks a lasting bond. The Heart and the Ring describe love moving toward commitment, such as a couple discussing a shared future, or a deep bond that comes full circle. Tip: speak plainly about what each of you means by commitment before any big step.",
"lnmpair:heart|book":"Love with something unspoken. The Heart and the Book suggest hidden feelings, a private affection, or someone still learning who you are. Picture a slow friendship that deepens as secrets are shared. Tip: reveal a little at a time and let trust set the pace.",
"lnmpair:heart|letter":"Warm words on the way. The Heart and the Letter point to a kind message, a love note, or an affectionate text. Think of a card that arrives when you needed it. Tip: write the thank-you or the honest kind message you have been postponing.",
"lnmpair:heart|man":"The person asking, or someone close, is led by affection. The Heart and the Man card show a person in a warm, open-hearted phase, or a loving figure in your life. Tip: let yourself receive care as well as give it, and say what you appreciate.",
"lnmpair:heart|woman":"Tenderness comes from you or someone near you. The Heart and the Woman card describe a caring, affectionate person, which might be you in a gentle stretch. Tip: treat your own needs with the same kindness you offer to others.",
"lnmpair:heart|lily":"Tender and peaceful. The Heart and the Lily point to gentle, mature affection, a love that feels safe and calm rather than dramatic. Think of an old friendship with no need to prove anything. Tip: enjoy the quiet, and protect time for it.",
"lnmpair:heart|sun":"Warm love in full daylight. The Heart and the Sun suggest joy, happy connection, and affection that is easy to express. Picture a cheerful gathering where everyone feels welcome. Tip: say yes to invitations, share the good mood, and let people see you glad.",
"lnmpair:heart|moon":"Feelings seen and honored. The Heart and the Moon describe emotional recognition, a sensitive and romantic mood, or being appreciated for who you are. Tip: trust the quiet signals from your gut, and make room for a tender conversation with someone who matters.",
"lnmpair:heart|key":"Affection finds an answer. The Heart and the Key point to a feeling that becomes clear, or a door opening between two people. In a question about a friendship, it can be the honest talk that settles things. Tip: say the warm thing plainly instead of hinting.",
"lnmpair:heart|fish":"Care meets flow here. The Heart and the Fish suggest work you love that also supports you, or generosity moving freely between people. A small example is a shop run with real affection that keeps customers returning. Tip: let appreciation be as visible as the numbers.",
"lnmpair:heart|anchor":"Steady love, the kind that stays put. The Heart and the Anchor describe an attachment built over years, a loyalty that holds in dull weeks as well as bright ones. It might be an old friend who still shows up. Tip: tend what is lasting before chasing something new.",
"lnmpair:heart|cross":"Love carrying weight. The Heart and the Cross read as devotion tied to duty, or a feeling that asks something hard of you, like caring for a relative. There is meaning in it, though it can tire you. Tip: share the load and let someone care for you back.",
"lnmpair:ring|book":"A promise with parts left unsaid. The Ring and the Book suggest an agreement you have not fully read, or a commitment whose details are still hidden. Think of signing up for something before learning the terms. Tip: study the fine print and ask the missing question first.",
"lnmpair:ring|letter":"Something formal in writing. The Ring and the Letter point to a contract, an invitation to commit, or a written agreement arriving or being drafted. A lease or a job offer is a fair picture. Tip: keep a copy and read it twice before answering.",
"lnmpair:ring|man":"The person this card stands for is tied to a commitment. The Ring and the Man show someone bound by a promise, a partnership or a repeating cycle. In a question, it may be your own pledge, or someone close to you. Tip: ask whether the bond still fits.",
"lnmpair:ring|woman":"A person bound to a promise or a cycle. The Ring and the Woman point to the one this card stands for, or someone close to you, being tied into an agreement or a partnership. Think of a business pair or a long-standing arrangement. Tip: check that both sides still want it.",
"lnmpair:ring|lily":"A bond that has settled into calm. The Ring and the Lily describe a commitment that has matured, with less drama and more ease. Picture a long partnership where nobody needs to prove anything anymore. Tip: appreciate that quiet and keep the agreements simple.",
"lnmpair:ring|sun":"A commitment that thrives. The Ring and the Sun suggest an agreement or partnership with good energy behind it, warm and openly celebrated. A joint project that finally gets credit is one example. Tip: say yes where the enthusiasm is mutual, and enjoy it.",
"lnmpair:ring|moon":"A pledge that touches feeling. The Ring and the Moon point to a commitment that earns recognition, or one driven by mood and intuition rather than logic. You might be asked to commit because of a gut sense. Tip: let feelings inform the choice without deciding it alone.",
"lnmpair:ring|key":"An agreement that unlocks something. The Ring and the Key suggest a commitment made with clarity, or a contract that opens a door you wanted open. Think of a signed deal that finally lets a plan begin. Tip: make sure the terms really give you what you need.",
"lnmpair:ring|fish":"A business agreement or a recurring flow. The Ring and the Fish point to contracts, partnerships and arrangements that run in cycles, such as a retainer or a regular client. Tip: write down who owes what, and review the arrangement before it renews.",
"lnmpair:ring|anchor":"A commitment built to last. The Ring and the Anchor suggest a promise you intend to keep for the long haul, with patience as its main ingredient. Think of a shared home or a decade-long partnership. Tip: choose commitments you are willing to maintain, not only begin.",
"lnmpair:ring|cross":"A promise that feels like a burden. The Ring and the Cross read as an obligation you accepted and now carry, or a commitment with a heavy sense of purpose. A caregiving arrangement is one picture. Tip: renegotiate what can be adjusted, and keep the part that matters.",
"lnmpair:book|letter":"A document holding knowledge. The Book and the Letter suggest a report, an article or a message with information you did not have before. Perhaps a reply that explains what has been unclear. Tip: read the whole thing slowly before reacting to any one line.",
"lnmpair:book|man":"The person this card stands for is learning or holding back. The Book and the Man show someone studying a subject, keeping a confidence, or not yet ready to reveal their plans. It may be you. Tip: give them room to finish what they are working out.",
"lnmpair:book|woman":"A student or a keeper of secrets. The Book and the Woman point to the person this card stands for, or someone close to you, gathering knowledge or guarding something private. Picture a quiet friend with a long project. Tip: be curious, not pushy.",
"lnmpair:book|lily":"Wisdom that comes with age. The Book and the Lily suggest calm study, a mentor, or knowledge held gently. Picture an older relative explaining how something really works. Tip: take your time with learning, and let understanding settle before you act on it.",
"lnmpair:book|sun":"A secret coming into the light. The Book and the Sun point to understanding that becomes clear, a study that pays off, or hidden information finally made public. Think of an exam you prepared for well. Tip: share what you learn, because it can help others too.",
"lnmpair:book|moon":"Intuition about something unknown. The Book and the Moon describe a hunch that points toward a hidden matter, or study that earns recognition. You may sense there is more to a story than you were told. Tip: write the feeling down and test it against facts.",
"lnmpair:book|key":"The answer to a puzzle. The Book and the Key suggest a missing piece of knowledge that unlocks a problem, such as the one fact that makes the instructions work. Tip: look for the source you skipped, the manual or the person who already knows.",
"lnmpair:book|fish":"Learning tied to livelihood. The Book and the Fish point to training, research or a hidden detail in your finances or business. An example is taking a course that opens a better line of work. Tip: look into the details before committing resources, and keep notes.",
"lnmpair:book|anchor":"Slow, patient learning. The Book and the Anchor suggest a long course of study, or a secret kept for years. Think of mastering a craft over many seasons. Tip: commit to a steady routine, and expect understanding to grow gradually instead of arriving overnight.",
"lnmpair:book|cross":"Heavy knowledge. The Book and the Cross read as a truth that is hard to carry, or study that feels like a burden. Maybe a difficult subject, or a confidence you wish you had not been given. Tip: find one person you can trust to talk it through with.",
"lnmpair:letter|man":"News arriving for the person this card stands for. The Letter and the Man suggest a message, an email or a document landing in your hands, or coming from a significant person. Tip: reply while the details are fresh, and keep the wording clear.",
"lnmpair:letter|woman":"A message tied to a significant person. The Letter and the Woman point to the one this card stands for, or someone near you, sending or receiving words in writing. It could be a long-awaited reply. Tip: put your side in writing too, so nothing gets lost.",
"lnmpair:letter|lily":"A calm, mature message. The Letter and the Lily suggest gentle words, a peaceful exchange, or a document that settles matters politely. Think of a thoughtful note from an older relative. Tip: answer in the same unhurried tone, and skip any urge to argue.",
"lnmpair:letter|sun":"Good news in writing. The Letter and the Sun point to a cheerful message, a positive result or a document that brings clarity and warmth. An acceptance email is the classic picture. Tip: share it with someone, and answer promptly while the energy is high.",
"lnmpair:letter|moon":"A message that stirs feeling. The Letter and the Moon suggest words that bring recognition, or a note read more emotionally than it was written. Perhaps a thank-you that lands deeply. Tip: pause before answering, since tone is easy to misjudge on a page.",
"lnmpair:letter|key":"A message that solves something. The Letter and the Key point to a clear reply, an official confirmation, or a document that opens the next step. Think of the email with the missing code. Tip: act on the instructions inside, and save the message.",
"lnmpair:letter|fish":"Paperwork about business or funds. The Letter and the Fish suggest an invoice, a statement, an offer or a message about trade. A client writing to confirm an order fits. Tip: check the figures yourself and keep records of what was said.",
"lnmpair:letter|anchor":"A message that makes things steady. The Letter and the Anchor suggest dependable communication, or a document with lasting weight, such as a deed or a standing agreement. Tip: file it somewhere safe, and keep up regular contact with whoever matters.",
"lnmpair:letter|cross":"A difficult message. The Letter and the Cross read as heavy news in writing, a formal notice, or words that carry an obligation. It can be a tough email you have been dreading. Tip: breathe, read it fully, then answer in plain and measured terms.",
"lnmpair:man|woman":"Two significant people meeting. The Man and the Woman show the person asking and another important person, any gender, side by side. In a question it asks how the two of you relate, as partners, colleagues or friends. Tip: notice who is leading and who is listening.",
"lnmpair:man|lily":"Maturity in a person. The Man and the Lily suggest the person this card stands for acting with calm, patience and good judgment, or an older, wiser presence nearby. Tip: take their lead on a delicate matter, and let tempers cool before talking.",
"lnmpair:man|sun":"Vitality and success around a person. The Man and the Sun point to the person this card stands for feeling confident, energetic and visible, or being warmly recognised. Picture a good week at work. Tip: use the momentum on something that matters to you.",
"lnmpair:man|moon":"A person guided by feeling. The Man and the Moon suggest the one this card stands for being emotionally open, intuitive, or in need of acknowledgment. Perhaps a talk about how someone really feels. Tip: listen for the mood behind the words.",
"lnmpair:man|key":"A person who holds the solution. The Man and the Key point to the one this card stands for finding clarity, making a decisive move, or being the one who opens a door. Tip: trust a straightforward plan, and ask directly for what you need.",
"lnmpair:man|fish":"A person at work in business and resources. The Man and the Fish suggest the one this card stands for dealing with trade, income or a flow of opportunities. An example is a freelancer building a client list. Tip: stay generous but keep your accounts tidy.",
"lnmpair:man|anchor":"A steady, committed person in the picture. The Man card and the Anchor together describe someone who stays put and sees things through, or you in that mode. Think of the colleague who has quietly held the same project for years. Tip: lean on that reliability, and notice if steadiness has turned into being stuck.",
"lnmpair:man|cross":"Carrying a heavy load, or being the one others lean on. The Man card beside the Cross shows a person weighed down by duty, or by something that feels meaningful but tiring. Picture a sibling managing a parent's affairs alone. Tip: name what is truly yours to carry, and ask for help with the rest.",
"lnmpair:woman|lily":"A calm, mature presence. The Woman card with the Lily points to a person who brings peace into the room, or to you at your most settled. Think of the one who defuses a tense family dinner with a few quiet words. Tip: choose patience over the quick reaction in this matter.",
"lnmpair:woman|sun":"Confidence and warmth in a person who shines easily. The Woman card and the Sun suggest someone energised, well regarded and good to be around, or you feeling that way. Picture the friend whose good mood lifts a whole office. Tip: step forward and let yourself be seen, because the light is on your side.",
"lnmpair:woman|moon":"A person who is noticed, and who feels deeply. The Woman card with the Moon points to emotional intuition and a wish for recognition. Example: a coworker whose gut sense about a client turns out to be right. Tip: trust the hunch, then tell someone about the effort behind it so it gets seen.",
"lnmpair:woman|key":"Someone holding the answer. The Woman card and the Key describe a person who unlocks a situation, or you finding clarity at last. Think of the friend who finally says the one sentence that makes the plan make sense. Tip: act on what is now clear instead of asking for yet another opinion.",
"lnmpair:woman|fish":"A person with a head for business and resources. The Woman card beside the Fish suggests someone resourceful, who keeps things flowing, such as a freelancer who juggles several clients smoothly. It can also describe you in that role. Tip: keep your records simple and tidy, and let the work circulate.",
"lnmpair:woman|anchor":"Loyalty that lasts. The Woman card with the Anchor shows a person who holds their ground and stays committed, or you choosing to dig in. Picture the neighbor who has run the same community garden for twenty years. Tip: stability is a strength here, but check that it still fits who you are now.",
"lnmpair:woman|cross":"A person under pressure, or searching for meaning in a hard stretch. The Woman card and the Cross describe someone shouldering responsibility, such as a caregiver juggling work and family. It may be you. Tip: write down what you are carrying, then decide which part you can set down this week.",
"lnmpair:lily|sun":"Warm, easy contentment. The Lily and the Sun together describe a good season of calm, mature happiness, the kind found in a relaxed Sunday with people you trust. Nothing is forced, and things feel clear. Tip: enjoy it without hunting for a catch, and let simple pleasures count.",
"lnmpair:lily|moon":"Quiet dignity that gets noticed. The Lily and the Moon point to a gentle, emotionally mature way of being recognised, praised for your tact instead of for volume. Picture a mediator thanked after a hard meeting. Tip: let your steadiness speak, and accept the appreciation that follows.",
"lnmpair:lily|key":"Peace comes through a clear, calm answer. The Lily and the Key suggest a matter settles when someone speaks plainly and kindly, such as a long family disagreement ending with one honest talk. Tip: choose the gentle, direct sentence over the clever one, and then stop explaining.",
"lnmpair:lily|fish":"Money matters handled with maturity. The Lily and the Fish describe a peaceful, steady flow in business, like a small shop with loyal regulars and no drama. Nothing flashy, just balanced. Tip: favor ethical, unhurried choices in work dealings, and let the calm pace be the plan.",
"lnmpair:lily|anchor":"Lasting peace. The Lily and the Anchor point to harmony that holds, such as a long partnership or friendship where both sides have grown comfortable and kind. Think of a couple's quiet evening routine. Tip: keep the habits that support this steadiness, and do not mistake calm for boredom.",
"lnmpair:lily|cross":"Serenity tested by duty. The Lily and the Cross suggest a peaceful nature facing a heavy obligation, such as staying composed while handling a relative's paperwork. Maturity is asked of you. Tip: keep your calm as a resource, not a mask, and set limits on what you absorb.",
"lnmpair:sun|moon":"Public recognition and real feeling arriving together. The Sun and the Moon describe being seen and valued, with the emotional side included. Example: a presentation that goes well and also feels meaningful. Tip: say yes to the spotlight when it comes, and check that your heart agrees with where it points.",
"lnmpair:sun|key":"A bright, certain opening. The Sun and the Key suggest a clear answer arriving with energy behind it, like a job decision that suddenly feels obvious and good. Doors seem to swing open easily. Tip: move while the momentum is strong, and take the step you have been rehearsing.",
"lnmpair:sun|fish":"Success in business and money flow. The Sun and the Fish point to a thriving venture, full of energy and visible results, such as a side project that begins attracting steady customers. Tip: keep your confidence, and keep your numbers honest so the good season has a solid base.",
"lnmpair:sun|anchor":"Success that lasts. The Sun and the Anchor describe achievement built on perseverance, the kind earned by showing up daily, like a team that keeps winning because it never skips practice. Tip: protect the routines behind the results, since they matter more than any single bright moment.",
"lnmpair:sun|cross":"Optimism meeting a burden. The Sun and the Cross suggest warmth and energy are being asked to carry something heavy, such as keeping a cheerful face during a demanding project. Tip: let the brighter mood lighten the load rather than hide it, and share the task with others.",
"lnmpair:moon|key":"Intuition that finds the answer. The Moon and the Key describe a feeling turning into certainty, like sensing which offer is right before the numbers confirm it. Recognition may also open a door. Tip: write down your instinct today, then test it gently against the facts.",
"lnmpair:moon|fish":"Feelings and money moving together. The Moon and the Fish suggest your intuition guides business, or that recognition brings work your way. Example: a post that gets noticed and leads to a client. Tip: pay attention to your mood when you decide on deals, and check the figures with a calmer head.",
"lnmpair:moon|anchor":"Emotions that want a steady place. The Moon and the Anchor point to a lasting feeling, or a need for security and routine, such as staying in a neighborhood because it feels like yours. Tip: build comforts that hold, but let sentiment inform your choices without freezing them.",
"lnmpair:moon|cross":"Emotional weight and a search for meaning. The Moon and the Cross describe heavy feelings, perhaps guilt or a sense of obligation that goes unrecognised. Picture quietly resenting a role nobody thanked you for. Tip: say aloud what you carry, and let someone acknowledge it.",
"lnmpair:key|fish":"An opening in business or money flow. The Key and the Fish suggest a solution arriving in work matters, such as a contract term clarified or a supplier problem solved. Resources start moving again. Tip: use the opening promptly and keep the paperwork clear so it stays open.",
"lnmpair:key|anchor":"A solution that lasts. The Key and the Anchor point to an answer that holds over time, like a repair done properly so it does not need redoing. Certainty becomes a firm footing. Tip: choose the thorough fix over the fast one, and commit to it.",
"lnmpair:key|cross":"Meaning unlocked, or a burden finally understood. The Key and the Cross describe finding the reason behind a hard duty, such as realising why a family role fell to you. Relief can follow clarity. Tip: ask what the obligation is teaching, then decide whether to keep it.",
"lnmpair:fish|anchor":"Steady, long-term income and business. The Fish and the Anchor suggest resources that stay reliable, like a small enterprise with repeat customers year after year. Growth is gradual and secure. Tip: favor patient, durable arrangements, and keep a modest reserve instead of chasing every new stream.",
"lnmpair:fish|cross":"Business with a heavy side. The Fish and the Cross point to money matters that feel like a duty or a strain, such as a tight month of bills and obligations. It can also mean work that carries real meaning. Tip: list your commitments plainly, and trim what no longer earns its place.",
"lnmpair:anchor|cross":"Enduring a heavy duty. The Anchor and the Cross describe long perseverance under pressure, such as caring for someone over many months with little rest. There is strength here, and also fatigue. Tip: keep your commitment, and arrange real breaks so the stamina lasts."};
